  note = {\textbf{Policy Brief No. 38, 2018}
\par
Decision Analysis to Support Agriculture
\par
for Nutrition
\par
\emph{Figure 1. The Decision Analysis modelling process involves a sequence of activities from identifying and framing}
\par
\emph{the decision to calculating uncertainty and high value variables in the resulting model. The process is implemented}
\par
\emph{in cooperation with key stakeholders and experts to improve the design of policy and interventions, and monitor}
\par
\emph{their impacts. The loop in the top half of the diagram describes the process that evaluates different alternatives in}
\par
\emph{relation to the decision goals, whereas the lower loop uses value of information analysis to determine what should be}
\par
\emph{measured to clarify the decision. There are iterative feedback loops throughout the process.}
\par
\textbf{Key Messages}
\par
{$\bullet$}
\par
{$\bullet$}
\par
{$\bullet$}
\par
{$\bullet$}
\par
{$\bullet$}
\par
There is need for new approaches in the
\par
analysis of impacts of agricultural interventions
\par
on food and nutrition systems.
\par
Making decisions on how to promote
\par
agriculture for nutrition requires approaches
\par
that can accommodate complex relationships
\par
and translate agricultural activities into
\par
probable nutritional outcomes.
\par
Decision Analysis is a set of methods designed
\par
to assess decisions and offer a recommended
\par
course of action for decision makers.
\par
Decision Analysis can be used to meet the
\par
challenges of system complexity and data
\par
scarcity, inherent in development decisions.
\par
The methods have been designed to support
\par
decision-making with imperfect information
\par
and limited research budgets.
\par
Bayesian Networks are a probabilistic decision
\par
modelling tool that can be used to integrate
\par
expert knowledge, considering system
\par
complexity and uncertainty to anticipate the
\par
future impacts of decisions.
\par
{$\bullet$}
\par
Decision modelling with Bayesian Networks
\par
can be applied to quantify the pathways from
\par
agricultural policies to their impacts on
\par
nutrition.
\par
\textbf{The Challenge}
\par
The Sustainable Development Goals include the
\par
commitment to end hunger and food insecurity and to
\par
improve global nutrition by 2030 (SDG 2; United Nations
\par
2015). Several possible interventions that might help to
\par
achieve these ambitious goals have been identified.
\par
Many of the identified interventions target agricultural
\par
production. The challenge for governments and
\par
institutions is to select the most effective among all of
\par
the possible interventions. The danger is that some of
\par
the proposed interventions may not produce positive
\par
outcomes at all, for instance certain foods may never
\par
reach vulnerable groups such as the rural poor.
\par
1\textbf{Decision-Making for an Uncertain}
\par
\textbf{Future}
\par
Research supporting development decisions related to
\par
agriculture for nutrition often fails to consider the range of
\par
ecological, socioeconomic, cultural and political factors
\par
that influence agriculture. This often limits decision
\par
makers' ability to rationally decide on the best course
\par
of action in developing and implementing agriculture for
\par
nutrition interventions.
\par
The basic steps in the Decision Analysis process aim to
\par
address both why and how decisions are formulated and
\par
decision-relevant factors are measured. This approach
\par
follows the basic premise that if something has an effect,
\par
this effect must be observable and if it is observable, it
\par
must be measurable.
\par
Through the stochastic and iterative modelling process
\par
Decision Analysis can supply decision makers with
\par
information about probable impact pathways of
\par
agricultural interventions and thereby help them prioritize
\par
for the best nutrition outcomes.
\par
\textbf{\emph{Bayesian Networks to Support Decisions}}
\par
A Bayesian Network is a modelling tool that can be
\par
used together with Decision Analysis methods to supply
\par
decision makers with information about the probable
\par
impacts of particular interventions. By capturing the
\par
interactions of a complex range of factors, these
\par
models can produce probabilistic projections of system
\par
outcomes. Bayesian Network models are particularly
\par
useful where exact data are limited but extensive expert
\par
knowledge is available, as is often the case in agricultural
\par
development.
\par
\textbf{Use of local expert knowledge}
\par
Decision Analysis techniques can help to
\par
accommodate complex relationships and
\par
integrate the knowledge of local experts for model
\par
construction and calculation. These can be used to
\par
build and parameterize Bayesian Networks.
\par
Once a decision has been identified, the modelling
\par
process begins by breaking the decision down into
\par
several important questions in plenary discussions.
\par
Random interchanging working groups of experts are
\par
then led through the various stages of collaborative
\par
thinking regarding these questions.
\par
The process is designed to help experts interact,
\par
brainstorm and reach a common understanding
\par
about impact pathways. Through this approach,
\par
they can explore details of the expected impacts,
\par
disaggregate the impact pathway into intermediate
\par
steps and identify all the influencing factors that
\par
they consider important to the decision.
\par
\emph{Figure 2. Example of an agriculture-for-nutrition-focused}
\par
\emph{Bayesian Network describing the nutrition impacts of}
\par
\emph{Uganda's Vision 2040 development decision for a nation-}
\par
\emph{wide transition from small-scale farming systems to}
\par
\emph{industrial agriculture. The model results indicate that}
\par
\emph{implementation of Vision 2040 creates a worrying increase}
\par
\emph{in the probability of a household experiencing hunger and}
\par
\emph{micronutrient deficiency. The data and model are available}
\par
\emph{on the Harvard DataVerse (Luedeling and Whitney 2017).}
\par
2\emph{Figure 3. Example of an agriculture-for-nutrition-focused Bayesian Network describing the impact pathway for}
\par
\emph{farmers' tree-planting related management decisions on diets on smallholder farms in Kenya. The decision to}
\par
\emph{plant trees was found to improve households' intakes of dietary energy, iron, vitamin A and zinc. The data and}
\par
\emph{the model are available on the Harvard DataVerse (Luedeling and Whitney 2018)}
\par
\emph{Figure 4. There are a number of steps that can be followed for collaborative building and calculating of decision impact}
\par
\emph{pathways. This process can be performed in a workshop where expert knowledge holders and analysts are brought}
\par
\emph{together to construct an impact pathway model of a particular decision (more information on this process can be found}
\par
\emph{in Whitney et al. 2018).}
\par
3\textbf{\emph{Decision Analysis to address uncertainty}}
\par
Decision Analysis models can help to address the inherent
\par
uncertainty in policy-related decision-making regarding
\par
linkages between agriculture and nutrition. By applying
\par
these methodologies, it is possible to create locally
\par
specific decision models that include relationships that
\par
may be difficult to capture in other modelling approaches.
\par
This approach demonstrates a way to involve local
\par
knowledge systems in decision analysis of nutrition
\par
impact pathways of agricultural production interventions.
\par
The approaches offer an alternative to ignoring factors
\par
that are difficult to measure when considering complex
\par
systems.
\par
