Abstract
\par
Purpose-- Building information modelling (BIM) provides a robust platform for information management in
\par
built environment facilities. However, one of the consequences of the limited application of BIM in facilities
\par
information management (FIM) is that the potential value gain through the integration of as-built information
\par
during the operations management process has had limited exploration in current practice. The purpose of
\par
this paper is to explore the potential impact of BIM to enhance the value of FIM.
\par
Design/methodology/approach-- A detailed literature review was undertaken to identify BIM application
\par
in a construction context, and to develop a framework to investigate the value of information. An
\par
interpretative approach was adopted for data collection and analysis. A total of 14 semi-structured interviews
\par
were conducted with construction industry professionals to identify how value of FIM can be enhanced
\par
through BIM integration. The interview data were analysed using open and selective coding.
\par
Findings-- The findings confirm that information exchange between the construction and facilities
\par
management (FM) phases of a project are important in terms of efficient and effective maintenance of a
\par
facility as well as optimising the design task. With these promising benefits, BIM is an efficient mechanism to
\par
facilitate construction information exchange. However, there is an uncertainty over the optimum level of
\par
information that ought to be on a BIM model for FM purposes. The relationship between different aspects of
\par
value is a starting point to filter the required information for each individual project. In contrast, limited
\par
awareness of value of information exchange and the potential of BIM enabled FIM during construction
\par
is noted.
\par
Research limitations/implications-- The information exchange considered within this investigation was
\par
limited to two key phases of the facility life cycle, namely, construction and FM (in-use).
\par
Practical implications-- The findings bring insight into an unseen aspect of FM information needs that
\par
should be given priority in upcoming BIM developments. Also, it draws attention to how value is concerned in
\par
a daily basis beyond monetary terms.
\par
Originality/value-- The investigation of value enhancement through BIM integration in particular to FIM
\par
and ongoing research with new value dimensions.
\par
Keywords Construction, Building information modelling, Information value, Facility information management
\par
Paper type Research paper
