\par
Although the application of BIM is dominant in design and construction phases, it is a
\par
promising process to improve through-life performance of a built asset (Codinhoto et al.,
\par
2013). Frequent adoption of BIM in early stages of construction facilities has been identified
\par
to give proven benefits, such as effective coordination, visualisation of the end product and
\par
early detection of clashes (Azhar, 2011). There are limited case studies to reveal the benefits
\par
of BIM beyond design and construction phases (CRC Construction Innovation, 2007; Arayici
\par
et al., 2012; Patacas et al., 2015). On the other hand, construction clients are more concerned
\par
about the end products (tangible deliverables), rather than the long-term operational and
\par
maintenance requirements (Becerik-Gerber et al., 2012).
\par
The success of building maintenance and operations (FM) depends on robust
\par
information management practices (NBS, 2015). The data that flow (in--out) during the life
\par
cycle of a construction facility are high in volume, complex and more frequent. The UK
\par
Government have been set clear targets to implement BIM Level 2 for all publicly procured
\par
projects by 2016. This promotes the collaboration between project stakeholders to enhance
\par
the through-life project performance.
\par
There are growing number of extant research papers explaining the benefits of
\par
implementing BIM in the FM tasks (Pittet et al., 2014; Giel and Issa, 2015) and the execution
\par
of BIM standards in construction (Howard and Bj\"ork, 2008). In a way, the current focus is
\par
mostly on``what'' could be achieved through BIM in FM. However, limited effort has been
\par
given on``how'' to achieve the BIM advantage in FM. Having identified this gap in the
\par
existing knowledge, this paper aims to review the perceived value of information used
\par
during FM and the potential of BIM to enhance the value of facilities information
\par
management (FIM). The term information management encompasses information,
\par
technologies, strategies and systems. However, the FIM considered within this paper is
\par
limited to the information management requirements during the FM phase of a built facility.
\par
2. FIM in construction projects
\par
Literature reveals construction as a heterogeneous and enormously complex industry.
\par
The industry produces one-off products, many of which have longer lifecycles. Supply chain
\par
integration has been identified as one of prominent considerations for effective delivery
\par
of construction products and processes. The project stakeholders have different levels of
\par
interests and influence towards the project goals. Therefore, the information flows of
\par
construction project are enormously complex, scattered and multi-dimensional.
\par
On the other hand, FM is the integration of processes within an organisation to maintain
\par
and develop the agreed services that support and improve the effectiveness of its primary
\par
activities (European Committee for Standardization, 2007). The FM process focusses on
\par
managing the complexities of a built facility to smooth the functioning of its physical
\par
structure and support services to enhance the core business performance (Kincaid, 1994).
\par
The information flows during FM stage is significant to achieve such targets. In a typical
\par
construction project, information exchange is frequent and the information providers/users
\par
range from design team, builders, suppliers, regulatory bodies, funders, end users, etc.
\par
Involvement of these roles on information exchange depends on the selected procurement
\par
route and the conditions of the contract (RIBA POW 2013). Therefore, information flows and
\par
the timing of them vary and are crucial to operate a facility with minimum disruptions.
\par
Having identified such variables, information considered within this paper is been narrowed
\par
down to as-built information of projects procured through a design and build arrangement.
\par
Also, it determines the``information value'' in terms of fit for purpose rather than monitory
\par
value due to several reasons such as a lack of experience in whole life application of BIM,
\par
unmeasured and indirect contribution of as-built information to FM and poor considerationof FM needs at the early stages of construction. However, it is visible that a successful FIM
\par
clearly considers the``type of information'' (WHAT),``timing of them'' (WHEN),``uses/
\par
functions'' (WHY),``flows'' (HOW) and the``users and providers'' (WHO). Therefore, it is vital
\par
to have a complete understanding beforehand, without being specific to the area of study.
\par
2.1 Types of information-- (WHAT)
\par
Based on existing knowledge, information which is used to complete FM functions comes in
\par
three main domains, namely,``Construction information''
\par
``Business information'' and
\par
,
\par
``Facility operation information''. Construction information refers to the information which is
\par
generated and in-use during the design and construction phases of a facility (Craig and
\par
Sommerville, 2006). Facilities managers identify construction information as the one which
\par
is exchanged between project manager and the owner/facility manager specifically to
\par
particular assets at the project handover stage (Clayton et al., 1999). For example, asset
\par
information, space allocations and maintenance schedules, etc.
\par
Business or market-driven information supports running the business, while
\par
maintaining the competitive advantage. For example, functional requirements related to
\par
the space being used, expected occupancy and other business functional information that
\par
influence facility operations are considered within this category. Usually, such information
\par
is fed into the FM system internally or externally (Chotipanich, 2004). The need is to have
\par
the right information at the right time to prosper the business.
\par
Facility operational information refers to the information which is directly related to the
\par
operation and maintenance of the facility in current and future environments. Examples of
\par
such information are energy consumption, maintenance records, facility operation staff
\par
information, etc. (Whitaker, 1995). Whitaker (1995) further noted the need of a proactive role
\par
of facility managers towards managing the operational information efficiently and
\par
productively.
\par
2.2 Timing of information (WHEN)
\par
Information needed for FM is generated and released in different phases of a project life
\par
cycle. For example, client information requirements are produced at preliminary stages of
\par
the facility, and they are continuously amended and improved during the design and
\par
construction stages. Although such information is generated during the inception phase, it
\par
may be released to the facilities manager along with the physical output of the project.
\par
Subsequently, asset operation and maintenance information such as post occupancy
\par
evaluations, breakdown records, energy consumption, etc., are added continuously during
\par
the FM phase. Information flows during the facility operations and maintenance stages
\par
include details of the built asset as well as the business operations. This continues up to the
\par
disposal of the facility at the end of its economic, physical or functional lifecycles, including
\par
information related to refurbishment or end of use.
