\par
812
\par
2.4 Users and providers (WHO)
\par
The FM-related information flows from a variety of sources. Construction information is
\par
provided by supply chain/stakeholders who are in the project team ranging from clients/end
\par
users, architects, engineers, service providers and manufacturers. Similarly, business
\par
operations information flows into the FM division from various departments within the
\par
organisation such as finance, marketing and administration as well as from external sources
\par
such as government, funders, standardisation bodies and competitors. Operations and
\par
maintenance information are mostly generated within the FM division. The users of FM
\par
information vary amongst different professional roles, and the level of the detail of the
\par
information used varies with the user and the project objectives.
\par
2.5 Flows (HOW)
\par
Business and facility operation information maintain a direct flow, as they are generated
\par
during the operation and maintenance of the facility on which FM have much more control
\par
over them. However, the construction information flows vary with the type of procurement
\par
arrangement. For example, in a design and build procurement approach, the responsibility
\par
of information handover is vested with the main contractor/architect creating a more
\par
centralised approach. In traditional procurement method (i.e. design-bid-build), it consists of
\par
a complex array of information flows due to the engagement of a number of parties and
\par
dispersed involvement in the construction phase.
\par
Having studied what, when, why, who and how questions, it is evident that information
\par
required for a continuous FM is scattered throughout the facility life cycle. Although an
\par
early engagement of a FM is recommended in theory, it is not frequent and visible in current
\par
practice (Eastman, 2011). The late involvement of facility managers in construction projects
\par
and the complexities in information flows create multiple difficulties for facilities managers
\par
when absorbing appropriate construction information (Clayton et al., 1999; Anderson et al.,
\par
2012; Wang et al., 2013). Hence, the information exchange between construction and FM
\par
stages is not coordinated. Literature further confirms that the root cause for most of the FIM
\par
issues are due to drawbacks arising from information generated and handed over from the
\par
construction phase (Clayton et al., 1999; Br\"ochner, 2008). Therefore, it is necessary to study
\par
the value of construction information for FM to understand the value of BIM in FIM. This is
\par
the way forward as BIM has the full capacity to carry information necessary for FM from
\par
the construction stage.
\par
3. Information value
\par
The term``value'' is multifaceted, providing different meanings to different stakeholders.
\par
Simply it is the``cost'' over ``benefits'', which represent the ultimate worth of the considered
\par
matter (Neal and Strauss, 2008). The costs and benefits can be communicated in different
\par
ways. Repo (1986) explained a dual approach as``exchange value'' and``value in use''
\par
.
\par
Exchange value refers to the market value of information when it is regarded as a product
\par
or service. On the other hand, value-in-use refers to the benefits of information to its users,
\par
which include both subjective and objective perspectives.
\par
However, the elements associated with each variable (objective or subjective) and the
\par
level of influence differ based on the project type, nature, etc. In construction, a balance of
\par
cost, quality and time is considered as a reasonable method to ascertain value (Best and
\par
De Valence, 1999). Although this paper mainly focusses on the architecture, construction,
\par
engineering and facilities management context, it is necessary to consider the features
\par
related to information to define the value of information beyond project success.
\par
Allocation of a monetary value to a piece of information is not always practical
\par
(Gallagher, 1974). From a case study analysis, Gavirneni et al. (1999) developed an equation
\par
to measure the value in supply chain information flow. The equation is integrated withthe monetary, performance and lead time improvements made through the availability of
\par
information. Similarly, by elaborating factors considered as benefits in value equation,
\par
Neal and Strauss (2008) introduced a measurement tool to capture the brand value. Both
\par
methods have been considered successful due to the structured nature of the manufacturing
\par
industry/products. On the other hand, what is measured in quantitative (monetary) methods
\par
as ``value'' of information is a single use of information and has always left with a question
\par
mark on what it is meant by``value''. However, key facts which could be taken forward to
\par
determine the value of information in FM are considered.
\par
Literature suggests several mechanisms to determine the value of information. The
\par
method which is popular in manufacturing industry compares two situations of performing
\par
a task with and without information (Gavirneni et al., 1999). However, value is something
\par
more ``adjectival rather substantive''; therefore, it should be considered along with object
\par
and interest (Perry, 1914). Moreover, Gallagher (1974) suggested three possible ways to
\par
measure the value of information. Among these three methods, measuring the value after
\par
the information is used and the consequences of the action are known as the best method to
\par
determine the value of information. Having considered such characteristics of value of
\par
information, this paper adopts``the combination of needs fulfilled by construction
\par
information towards achieving the FM functions'' as the value of information. Thus, the
\par
value-in-use is considered as its concerns fit with the characteristics of FM information,
\par
which assists to understand the uses of information within FM. This paper argues how
\par
current construction practices evaluate the value of information and how recent IT-led
\par
information management mechanisms influence on enhancing such values.
