4. BIM in AECO environments
\par
BIM is identified as one of``emerging approaches to design, construction, and facilities
\par
management, in which a digital representation of the building process is used to facilitate
\par
the information exchange and interoperability in a digital format'' (Eastman, 2011). In a
\par
construction context, it appears as an architectural, engineering, construction and
\par
operations (AECO) digital modelling process (McGraw-Hill, 2009; Succar, 2009). BIM is also
\par
introduced as a technology based process, which aims to enhance the performance of a built
\par
asset with through-life considerations (Love et al., 2013). In the context of FM, it holds more
\par
credit as a process; however, there is an ongoing debate on whether BIM is a mere``process''
\par
or a ``tool/technique'' (British Institute of Facilities Management, 2012). Literature explains a
\par
variety of definitions for BIM in AECO environments. Having recognised the holistic nature
\par
of construction, this paper considers BIM as``a set of interacting policies, processes and
\par
technologies generating a methodology to manage the essential building design and project
\par
data in digital format throughout the building's life-cycle'' (Penttil\"a, 2006). BIM is considered
\par
one of the mechanisms which assist project stakeholders on informed decision making and
\par
apposite coordination by providing correct information at the right time which may lead
\par
towards waste reduction (Love et al., 2013). However, limited attention has been paid to
\par
identify the whole life benefits of BIM in AECO environments.
\par
There is evidence to confirm that the project stakeholders are under pressure to adopt
\par
BIM within their project execution plans (Barlish and Sullivan, 2012). On the other hand,
\par
Love et al. (2013) explain BIM as a complication as it continues expanding from software to
\par
asset information management (AIM). However, BIM should not be treated as mere
\par
technology, adopted during design and construction stages of any construction asset.
\par
Attention should be extended to identify the through-life application and the organisational-
\par
wide implications of BIM when it comes to AIM. The need is to consider both FM and asset
\par
information in the early stages of the project life cycle (at least the design phase) to facilitate
\par
a continuous FM. However, there is no clear evidence on what type of FM information
\par
should be integrated with the BIM model, how to include such information and the valueECAM
\par
27,4
\par
814
\par
attached to such information within the existing construction/AIM practices. Therefore, a
\par
clear gap in the existing knowledge is identified.
\par
FM deals with an enormous amount of asset information, including acquiring, updating
\par
and analysing of such information (Wang et al., 2013). BIM, as a platform evolving from the
\par
early stages of a building, is a perfect solution for FM data management. BIM allows
\par
communication with FM needs in the early stages of the projects (British Institute of
\par
Facilities Management, 2012). The positive contribution of adopting BIM in FIM is identified
\par
as a significant value addition (Gu and London, 2010). Eadie et al. (2013) explain that
\par
facilities managers and clients benefit the most out of BIM implementation. Becerik-Gerber
\par
et al. (2012) argue that considerable effort should be given to define client's FM needs at the
\par
project briefing stage. However, the majority of the BIM enabled projects are reluctant to
\par
handover the complete 3D model and Construction Operations Building Information
\par
Exchange (COBie) data sets at the commissioning stage of such built assets, and this
\par
prevents BIM adoption in FM (Eadie et al., 2013).
\par
COBie and Industry Foundation Classes (IFC) are the most popular standard formats for
\par
data exchange within BIM environment. In terms of FM, COBie is seen as a neutral
\par
spreadsheet format which allows data exchange in a structured manner for commissioning,
\par
operation and maintenance of an asset (British Standards Institute, 2012). IFC is another
\par
common language for information sharing (Abanda et al., 2015), which provides a standard
\par
form of data sharing between construction, operations and maintenance stages of a built
\par
asset (International Standard Organisation, 2013). IFC does not provide clear details on what
\par
information should be claimed to perform any specific task under a given scope (East et al.,
\par
2013). It is a format to store data to promote interoperability of information. Limited
\par
understanding of the appropriate information requirement for continuous FM, and the level
\par
of details of these information, are identified as the two key factors for limited
\par
implementation of BIM within FM (Parsanezhad and Dimyadi, 2014). This evidenced the
\par
need for robust mechanisms to acquire necessary information for FM. Correct identification
\par
of the value that attaches to facilities information is a mainstream mechanism to filter
\par
necessary information (Zhao et al., 2008).
\par
5. Research methodology
\par
A preliminary literature review was undertaken to identify the existing knowledge gap and
\par
to formulate the research question. At the second stage, a snowballing approach was
\par
adopted to carryout interviews to study the context and main themes, which are``value of
\par
information''
\par
,
\par
``facilities management'' and``application of BIM in FM functions''
\par
.
\par
The research disqualifies the pure quantitative approach due to the lack of established
\par
knowledge on BIM in FM and value of facilities information (Bosch et al., 2015). Therefore,
\par
priority was given to adopt the interpretative approach to mine the data in detail. A total of
\par
14 semi-structured interviews were conducted among construction industry professionals
\par
who were engaged in facilities information demand and supply processes. These interviews
\par
were aimed at identifying FM information needs, value of facilities information and further
\par
to explain how such values can be enhanced through BIM integration. The collected data
\par
were analysed through open and selective coding and structured through NVivo 11.
\par
6. Data collection and analysis
\par
Snowball sampling method was used to select the suitable participants for the interviews.
\par
This sampling method provides the opportunity to gather data from an appropriate
\par
population under the theory of those members with a special relevance are familiar with
\par
each other in the population (Penrod et al., 2003).
\par
The process of FIM consist of several phases (i.e. information requirement identification,
\par
communication of these information requirements to project stakeholders, handover anduse/reuse of information). Also, the information flows are complicated as it flows to and
\par
from different parties. In this regard, the interview sample was selected to cover both
\par
information supply and demand/user sides. Altogether, 14 semi-structured interviews were
\par
conducted with five facilities managers (FM1-5), two estate managers (EM 1-2), two
\par
contractors (C1-2), two architects (A1-2), one building surveyor (BS), one BIM manager (BM)
\par
and a CAFM service provider (SP). All the interviewees have more than seven years of
\par
professional experience. Priority was given to collecting data from information users ( five
\par
facility managers, two estate managers, one BIM manager and one CAFM service provider)
\par
due to the limited application of BIM within FM. Data collection continued until data
\par
saturation was obtained.
\par
The interview questionnaire was designed and piloted for its clarity and readability.
\par
Having noted the comments given in the pilot run, a few amendments were made into the
\par
interview questionnaire. The questionnaire consisted of open ended and multiple-choice
\par
questions, and they were structured into four main sections. The first section was aimed at
\par
identifying the generic information of interviewees such as industry experience, type of
\par
organisation, roles and responsibilities, etc., to prequalify the candidates and to identify any
\par
patterns based on the background of the respondent. The second section was targeted at
\par
identifying current FIM practices (including BIM and non-BIM environments), while the
\par
third section considered the fit for purpose of such information in achieving FM functions.
\par
The final section of the interview questionnaire was used to determine the value
\par
considerations of such information in achieving FM tasks, and further to study how BIM
\par
enhances the value of FIM. Each interview lasted between 40 min to 1 hour, and probe
\par
questions were considered where appropriate. All interviews were recorded using a digital
\par
recorder and transcribed for analysis.
\par
The purpose of qualitative data analysis is to break the data into smaller components to
\par
describe what the data are referring to, and to identify the concepts and their relationships
\par
(Dey, 1993). For this purpose, coding is an accepted initial data analysis tool in qualitative
\par
analysis (Berg, 2007). Qualitative coding involves defining the meaning of data and tagging
\par
them with an abstract name (Charmaz, 2006). A wide range of tools have been introduced for
\par
this purpose such as conceptual analysis, thematic analysis (Vaismoradi et al., 2013), pattern
\par
coding, cross case analysis (Yin, 2014), open, axial and selective coding (Corbin and Strauss,
\par
2008). Having analysed the merits and demerits of each method, the open, axial and selective
\par
coding were adopted within this investigation as it takes a step by step coding process to
\par
identify the emerging theories.
\par
Identification of information requirements was considered as one of the mandatory
\par
requirements to adopt BIM and for continuous FIM. The results explain that the influence
\par
(standards, technology, policies, etc.), quality (accuracy, usability, availability, completeness,
\par
format, etc.), sources (external, internal, etc.), types (essential, performance enhance, future
\par
potential use) of information were key considerations of FIM requirements. To ensure a
\par
better use of BIM in FIM, these considerations should be taken into account in advance. Any
\par
advancement in these areas would be a positive contribution to promote whole-life cycle
\par
BIM practices. The nodes generated through interview analysis are illustrated in Figure 1.
\par
The interview findings further helped to understand the different viewpoints of
\par
information users (client, facilities managers, estate managers, building surveyors, BIM
\par
manager, etc.) and the providers (contractors, sub-contractors, etc.) on current FIM practices.
\par
The information users claim``we get a mix of information [\dots ]. not all information is
\par
accurate a lot of the time'' (BS, FM3);``the lead contractor is accountable to provide necessary
\par
documents in soft formats at the handover'' (EM1). The information users expect that the
\par
providers will provide a complete set of valid information at the handover stage. However,
\par
information providers believe``the client needs to specify his information needs [\dots ]
\par
communicate the format they prefer. In most cases this won't happen'' (C1, C2 and CAFM).
