\par
project objectives. In construction, the project success is measured in time, cost and quality
\par
criteria, and most of the successful projects have considered at least two of them, although it
\par
is rare for time, cost and quality to be equal in either importance or impact. The business
\par
case would identify which criteria are most important and which would constitute the
\par
highest risk. This will impact in setting the project objectives. Similarly, the uses of
\par
construction information and the way they value it within the FM context also vary from
\par
project to project.
\par
Having analysed the interview findings, the value of information was integrated in to the
\par
current FIM (see Figure 3). The model considered three main considerations of information
\par
(i.e. value, types and uses); however, the priority of each consideration ranged from project
\par
settings to wider organisational settings. For example, information certainty is a high value
\par
concern in project environments; however, commercial value brings more competitive
\par
advantages in organisational environments. The area covered by each triangle
\par
demonstrates the FM information needs in both project and organisational environments
\par
and the magnitude of each triangle varies with the project/organisational objectives and
\par
goals. However, this needs to be tested in a real case scenario to identify how different
\par
projects determine value, and how those values vary in BIM and non-BIM environments.
