6.2 Uses of information
\par
The construction information is reviewed concerning their fit for purpose of achieving FM
\par
functions. Facilities managers prefer to have essential information as it helps the operation
\par
of the building at the beginning until the technical staff get used to the features of the
\par
facility. This is considered as the basic, yet most important, use of as-built information.
\par
The information gathered here is used to understand the operations of the building with
\par
minimum disruptions including maintenance requirements, equipment handling and
\par
possible precautions to be taken at any failure. The secondary functions aim at performing
\par
FM functions efficiently. Performance enhancement information tends to avoid time/money
\par
spend on physical inspection and costly consultancy surveys to collect necessary details
\par
about the built asset. Therefore, another application of as-built information is to use
\par
available information to make process improvements. At this point, a simple comparison
\par
between the types of information and its uses reveals that both are heading in the same
\par
direction (which is identified as value). Third, information is used to retain knowledge and
\par
ultimately gain confidence by in-depth knowledge of the facility. This is merely a subjective
\par
use of information; hence, no practical application is visible. In other words, information
\par
users prefer more information although they have no expected application but merely to
\par
gain a psychological satisfaction of being risk/doubt free.6.3 Value of information
\par
The term``value'' within the paper refers to the needs fulfilled by construction information in
\par
achieving the FM functions. The most valued fulfillment of information is its ability to
\par
create secure environments in both project and organisational levels. For example, when
\par
employees are informed about the characteristics, procedures and risks, they are more likely
\par
to be familiarised with the workplace and the work they do. On the other hand, information
\par
contributing to enhancing the product performance, and as a result creating value, is
\par
categorised under``performance value'' category. As noted by FM2``performance
\par
enhancement information is valuable through the use of the efficiency it brings in but.. it is
\par
preferred to gather information with potential application as it brings value through giving
\par
confidence about an unpredictable future''. The visible contribution of information such as
\par
time and cost savings, avoidable risks by being informed and support on decision making is
\par
also considered as a value addition of construction information. If any information provides
\par
a good market value with a high competitive advantage, such information is structured
\par
under a commercial value category. Potential benefits could be gained through information
\par
in the property market at the reselling stage or being accredited for best practice can be
\par
given as examples for``commercial value''. Within the construction context, information acts
\par
an important role as visible proof providing evidence to past activities.
\par
6.4 Value gain through BIM integration
\par
Frequent application of BIM in design and construction stages of a construction project, and
\par
its limited application during FM stage is identified. As a result, the information
\par
management during FM is challenging, and has been identified as a significantly time- and
\par
cost-consuming process. The UK Government's soft landing, which was initiated through
\par
the Construction Strategy 2025, is an attempt to provide a solution to some extent by
\par
pushing forward the involvement of the construction team further down in the facility life
\par
cycle. However, the practitioners identify the information diffusion as one of the significant
\par
reasons for making FIM somewhat complex. They suggest BIM as an ideal solution to
\par
integrate the required information into a single model, which makes FM information
\par
management easier than the traditional FIM. However, industry is lacking such strategies
\par
for BIM adoption during FM stage, and use firewalls around information models in an
\par
adversarial way.
\par
A complete BIM model provides a set of multiple information, which can be used
\par
in visualisation, energy modelling, simulations, life cycle costing and also to predict the
\par
performance of the facility during its FM phase. However, FM1 states,``BIM is still
\par
quite new in terms of industry awareness and the standards are only just starting to come
\par
through (e.g. PAS1192-3) so that you've got to catch it very early and you've got to have a
\par
lot of clarity very early to make sure that everybody who's worked on the project is putting
\par
the right stuff in the right format and the right structure at the right time to ensure
\par
a continuous FM''
\par
.
\par
BIM models usually allow assignment of information into different groups/variables,
\par
which will help to minimise the information redundancies. In fact, storing bulk information
\par
is costly for the owner, but the BIM process provides the possibility to filter the necessary
\par
information based on information value determined by the project stakeholders with the
\par
employer information requirement. Moreover, BM explains,``BIM has a greater ability to
\par
rapidly generate revision and to work through optioneering and scenarios which may
\par
highly benefit in whole life cost considerations of a facility''. In contrast, FM3 noted that
\par
``there's a big caveat around that because what we find is that where the project has been
\par
delivered and, where there are agreed structures and protocols around how the model would
\par
be functioned, then it is considerably more efficient. Where something goes wrong with that
\par
there are times when working with BIM can be less efficient because the model has to be
