\par
In summary, BIM is a process of initiating information management from the early stages
\par
of a built facility. This has become a partial solution to the problems faced during the design
\par
and construction stages in relation to gathering information from multiple stakeholders (Grilo
\par
and Jardim-Goncalves, 2010). It holds a greater potential in information management during
\par
FM (Giel and Issa, 2015). However, the potential benefits of BIM in FM are frequently gained
\par
through the information availability at the facility handover (Anderson et al., 2012). Therefore,
\par
it is necessary to identify FM information requirements at the early stages of the built facility.
\par
Due to the large number of information generated during design and construction, and the
\par
variety of needs in facility operation and maintenance, it is required to establish a robust
\par
information exchange. In this regard, the assessment of the value of information helps the
\par
users to identify their information requirement for FIM.
\par
7. Results and discussion
\par
The UK Government's contribution towards adoption of BIM in construction businesses is
\par
showing continuous growth over the past decade. Although it is mandatory to adopt BIM
\par
Level 2 in all publicly procured construction projects from 2016 onwards, its adoption is still
\par
dominant in the design and construction stages (Eadie et al., 2013; Love et al., 2013; Volk
\par
et al., 2014). With this influence, BIM is still recognised as mere technology used during
\par
design and construction stages, and has only limited knowledge and experience on its
\par
impact on the future of the organisation as an information tool for operation and
\par
maintenance of built asset over its life cycle (Love et al., 2013).
\par
How BIM enhances value in FIM is BIM facilitates initial data for, and information
\par
enhances the potentials of FM (Becerik-Gerber et al., 2012). Therefore, the success of BIM in
\par
FM depends on the information carried through BIM into FM. This has become a good
\par
reason to motivate the early engagement of facilities managers. In contrast, the late
\par
involvement of the facilities manager has restricted the opportunity of communicating FM
\par
information needs at the early stages of design and construction. Due to this reason, the
\par
facilities manager has to deal with the issues related to incomplete or inaccurate information
\par
handover. Communicating FM requirements at the early stages will accumulate operations
\par
and maintenance needs and enable the application of BIM in FM. Moreover, it is not only
\par
beneficial for information users but also for information providers as this communication
\par
cycle clears-out the client's requirement which is always a problematic situation from the
\par
information suppliers' perspective. Therefore, promoting an early engagement of the
\par
facilities manager is beneficial to the client as well as for the entire project team.
\par
Having analysed the demand side of information (users), it was noted that facilities
\par
managers should have a complete knowledge about the project and also clear awareness on
\par
deciding which information needs to be fed into the BIM platform for long-term use.
\par
Although BIM is technically capable of handling high volumes of information, it is
\par
important to understand the value of information especially at the early adoption stage.
\par
With the common view of information as something that is always valued, information
\par
users tend to demand``all the information'' which refers to much but says nothing. On the
\par
other hand, demanding``all the information'' is not helpful for information suppliers to
\par
understand the client requirement nor benefits to the facilities manager. This has been the
\par
most common reason to hold back BIM from FM in recent studies since no single person is
\par
completely aware of the exact applications of information exchange through BIM in FM.
\par
However, when identifying the role of construction information, it was clear that each type
\par
of information has specific uses as well as a unique value created through the availability
\par
and use of information. Yet, there is no structured frame for the types of information, its
\par
application and value based on the project objectives.The model introduced here guides the users on expected value addition through BIM
\par
while deciding what information should be asked from whom, when and why. This creates a
\par
win-win solution by informing the information requirements with their potential uses and
\par
value addition to the project team, since it is helpful to provide robust information at the
\par
handover as well as to have a clear understanding over client requirements.
