8. Conclusion
\par
The construction sector should focus on``product-service delivery'' rather than looking at
\par
the products and services separately. The limited awareness of BIM and its wider potentials
\par
by the client and facilities managers is the main barrier for adoption of BIM within FM
\par
(Giel and Issa, 2015). The initial motivation to adopt BIM in FM highly depends on the
\par
quality of information passed through the BIM processes. Considering the fact that having
\par
information is always useful, facilities managers tend to request all possible information
\par
about the built asset. Information suppliers conclude the reason for this as information
\par
users' poor understanding about the required information. Therefore, it is necessary to
\par
understand the specific uses of information to cooperate with a high-tech information
\par
society. If not, BIM would be misused as another type of software that replaces the
\par
maintenance documents storeroom.
\par
The most common scenario related to handover information in non-BIM environments is
\par
either the complete absence of as-built information or having incomplete information which
\par
has little use. On the other hand, the issue in a BIM environment is having a complex set of
\par
as-built information, yet not using most of it after a considerable investment on BIM
\par
process. The role of the proposed information model is to serve as a guiding tool by
\par
achieving the balance between FM information requirement and the value of information.
\par
More importantly, it is influential in mapping the work of FM and information with an
\par
organisation's objectives to make a blueprint of value created through information.
\par
In conclusion, from the information users' perspective, the model could be used to
\par
understand the information requirements in FM and what uses are to be obtained out of
\par
available information to create value. From the information suppliers' side, it provides a
\par
clear understanding of the client information requirements, thereby leading to quality
\par
improvements in handover information.
\par
Alongside the above-mentioned approach to establishing a model to ascertain the value
\par
of information, uptake of BIM in FM contexts is crucial to the overall success of FIM.
\par
However, at present, there is a paucity of coherent and robust evidential trial of what the
\par
value of BIM implementation is to FIM. As a result, current justification of BIM adoption is
\par
mostly based on perceived savings of through-life costs and value, rather than on what has
\par
actually being saved. Therefore, further research on establishing the quantum of savings
\par
(in terms financial, waste, processes, etc.) that can be accumulated from BIM implementation
\par
will be of outmost importance.
