Abstract
\par
Few if any natural resource systems are completely understood and fully observed. Instead,
\par
there almost always is uncertainty about the way a system works and its status at any given
\par
time, which can limit effective management. A natural approach to uncertainty is to allocate
\par
time and effort to the collection of additional data, on the reasonable assumption that more
\par
information will facilitate better understanding and lead to better management. But the col-
\par
lection of more data, either through observation or investigation, requires time and effort that
\par
often can be put to other conservation activities. An important question is whether the use of
\par
limited resources to improve understanding is justified by the resulting potential for improved
\par
management. In this paper we address directly a change in value from new information col-
\par
lected through investigation. We frame the value of information in terms of learning through
\par
the management process itself, as well as learning through investigations that are external
\par
to the management process but add to our base of understanding. We provide a conceptual
\par
framework and metrics for this issue, and illustrate them with examples involving Florida
\par
scrub-jays (\emph{Aphelocoma coerulescens}).
\par
\textbf{Introduction}
\par
Few if any natural resource systems are completely understood and fully observed. Instead, an
\par
almost universal situation is for there to be uncertainty about the way a system works and its
\par
status at any given time, which can limit effective management (Williams and Johnson [1]). A
\par
natural approach to uncertainty is to allocate time and effort to the collection of data, on the
\par
assumption that more information will facilitate better understanding and lead to better man-
\par
agement. But the collection of more data, either through observation or investigation, requires
\par
time and effort that could be put to other activities like conservation on the ground. An impor-
\par
tant question is whether the use of limited resources to improve understanding is justified by
\par
the potential to improve management (Doremus [2]). This question is often asked by manag-
\par
ers but only infrequently if ever answered satisfactorily, though some authors (see, e.g.,
\par
-MaAllister and Pikitch [3] and McAllister and Kirkwood [4]), have used expected resource
\par
valuations to contrast different monitoring strategies.
\par
PLOS ONE \textbar{} https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0199326 June 29, 2018 1 / 16Value of sample information in structurally uncertain resource systems
\par
