\par
There is by now a well-developed theory and approach for the assessment of the value of infor-
\par
mation in decision making. Raiffa and Schlaifer [5] provided one of the first seminal treatments
\par
for the value of information (VOI), coining the name and developing many of its key expressions.
\par
Since then many publications have offered descriptions of the value of information, (e.g., Quirk
\par
[6], Dakins et al. [7], Yakota and Thompson [8--9], Canessa et al. [10], Williams and Johnson
\par
[11]). Keisler et al. [12] provide a comprehensive review of applications of value of information
\par
analyses. Several metrics for the value of information are recognized (Yakota and Thompson [8]):
\par
{$\bullet$} The expected value of perfect information utilizes an average of optimal model-specific val-
\par
ues, averaged over the model likelihoods. The metric consists of this average net of the opti-
\par
mal value in the presence of process uncertainty.
\par
{$\bullet$} The expected value of partial information concerns the value added by eliminating uncer-
\par
tainty from a single source, assuming there is more than one source of uncertainty.
\par
{$\bullet$} Finally, the expected value of sample information expresses the potential gain in value from
\par
the collection of less than perfect information, using a comparison of optimal valuation with
\par
additional information against valuation in its absence. A general framework for the value of
\par
information that includes perfect, partial and sample information in sequential decision
\par
making for natural resources is described by Williams et al. [13].
\par
The expected value of perfect information has been used in a growing number of applica-
\par
tions in natural resource management (e.g., Conroy at al. [14], Ma \textasciidieresis ntyniemi et al. [15], Wil-
\par
liams et al. [13]), and several applications address the expected value of partial information
\par
(e.g., Moore and Runge [16], Johnson et al. [17], Maxwell et al. [18], and Johnson et al. [19]).
\par
On the other hand, the number of examples addressing the expected value of sample informa-
\par
tion is more restricted (e.g., Runge et al. [20], Moore et al. [21], Grantham et al. [22]). Few
\par
VOI applications in natural resources deal with dynamic resource systems, in which actions
\par
are dependent both on the state of the system and the degree of uncertainty in system dynam-
\par
ics (e.g., Shea et al. [23], Williams and Johnson [11,24], and Moore et al. [21]). Somewhat sur-
\par
prisingly, there are almost no examples for dynamic systems that address the expected value of
\par
sample information, even though many resource problems are fundamentally dynamic and a
\par
typical monitoring situation involves production of less than perfect information.
\par
In this article we address a change in value from sample information collected during the
\par
investigation in dynamic decision making. We frame the value of information in terms of
\par
learning through the management process itself, as well as learning through investigations that
\par
are external to management but add to our base of understanding. Our objective is to extend
\par
valuation to include dynamic decision making with sources of data that are both internal and
\par
external to the management process. The framework developed here goes beyond current
\par
treatments of the value of sample information in the literature, in its emphasis on management
\par
and learning about dynamic natural resources.
\par
In what follows the value of information is described in a context of sequential decision
\par
making under uncertainty, with future resource conditions and future understanding poten-
\par
tially influenced by current decisions. We focus specifically on structural uncertainty, that is,
\par
uncertainty about the processes that control resource dynamics. Partial observability (Williams
\par
[25]), another recognized and important source of uncertainty, can also be addressed by con-
\par
sidering additional resources to improve estimates of resource status. However, we emphasize
\par
structural uncertainty in this paper, and point the reader to expositions in the literature on val-
\par
uation under partial observability (Fackler [26], Williams and Johnson [24] and references
\par
therein). We provide two examples of the value of sample information based on the manage-
\par
ment of habitat for the Florida scrub-jay (Aphelocoma coerulescens).
\par
PLOS ONE \textbar{} https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0199326 June 29, 2018 2 / 16Value of sample information in structurally uncertain resource systems
\par
Decisions, returns, and uncertainty
\par
Among other things the value associated with sequential decision making under process or
\par
structural uncertainty depends on the amount of that uncertainty. With greater understanding
\par
one can make more informed (and higher valued) decisions; with less understanding progress
\par
toward achieving resource goals and objectives is limited.
\par
Here we assume a managed natural resource (e.g., a landscape, an amphibian population, a
\par
butterfly colony, the number of vegetative organism in an area) that is subject to only partial
\par
understanding. Uncertainty about how the resource system works is expressed by means of
\par
different hypotheses (models) about the system and its responses to management actions.
\par
Each model has a measurable likelihood of being the most appropriate, based on current infor-
\par
mation and understanding (Williams et al. [27]).
\par
We also assume a range of different management actions (e.g., different seeding mixes, har-
\par
vest strategies, water control regimes, geographic locations), with time-specific actions influ-
\par
encing the transition of the resource from its current state to a future state, and generating
\par
returns that provide a basis for comparing different management actions. Once an action is
\par
taken and a transition is made to a new state, another action is taken, and another return is
\par
generated at that time. The trajectory of anticipated returns depends on which hypothesis
\par
(model) is most appropriate, and therefore inherits the model uncertainty.
\par
The challenge in such a situation is to recognize and measure the change in value resulting
\par
from an increase in information and understanding. A broadly accepted measure of change is
\par
given by a comparison of optimal valuation produced with additional information, against
\par
optimal valuation in its absence (Raiffa and Schlaifer [5]). An understanding of the change in
\par
value enables assessment of cost-effectiveness in targeting uncertainty with additional research
\par
or monitoring.
\par
