Example: Habitat management for the Florida scrub-jay
\par
The Florida scrub-jay is an endemic species that is designated as threatened under the En-
\par
dangered Species Act (Root [37], Stith et al. [38]). Scrub-jays are restricted to Florida scrub
\par
(hereafter, ``scrub''), which is a rare habitat characterized by evergreen, xeromorphic shrubs
\par
including oaks, repent palms (Serenoa repens, Sabal etonia), and ericaceous shrubs (Lyonia
\par
spp., Vaccinium spp.) (Foster and Schmalzer [39]). Scrub is maintained by frequent fire, and
\par
landscape fragmentation and fire suppression have resulted in many scrub communities that
\par
are no longer capable of supporting scrub-jay populations (Breininger and Carter [40]). Pre-
\par
scribed burning has thus become the primary management tool in reserves where the viability
\par
of scrub-jays and other scrub species is an important objective.
\par
Of the many scrub attributes affecting scrub-jay demography (Breininger et al. [41]), per-
\par
haps the most important is scrub height (Breininger et al. [42], Breininger and Carter [40]).
\par
Scrub height is classified as short ({$<$}120 cm), optimal (120--170 cm), or tall-mix ({$>$}170 cm)
\par
(Breininger and Carter [40]). Short and optimal height scrub are further classified as open
\par
({$>$}50\% of the scrub containing bare ground) or closed. Optimal-height scrub acts as a reliable
\par
source habitat for jays, whereas the other classes always act as demographic sinks (Breininger
\par
and Oddy [43]). The goal of a manager is to maximize the cumulative demographic perfor-
\par
mance of scrub-jays over time, net the cost of conducting prescribed burns.
\par
For the purposes of this example, we assume a management unit that is homogenous, with
\par
one-year transition probabilities for each scrub class along with do-nothing and prescribed-
\par
burn management actions (S1 File). We also allow for an intensive burn to ensure that the
\par
entire management unit is effectively burned. Our null model posits that routine and intensive
\par
burns are equally effective (or ineffective) at setting back succession, though an intensive burn
\par
is more expensive due to the need to guard against greater threats to infrastructure and public
\par
safety. Thus, an intensive burn is never optimal under the null model. The alternative model
\par
posits that intensive burns are more effective at setting back succession than routine burns,
\par
and thus would be used when their greater short-term cost is offset by greater demographic
\par
performance of scrub-jays over the long term. The optimal, actively adaptive policy is depicted
\par
in Fig 2, in which the optimal management action is a function of both scrub state (i.e., system
\par
state) and the probability of the null model (i.e., model state). The optimal action can be an
\par
intensive burn as long as there is at least some probability (\dbend 0.002) of the alternative model
\par
being correct. But even in those cases, an intensive (and more expensive) burn is only optimal
\par
for the most fire-resistant states (short-closed, optimal-closed, and tall-mix). State and action-
\par
specific transition probabilities and returns, and computational details for the actively adaptive
\par
policy are provided in Supporting Information (S1 File).
\par
In this example we assume that data external to the management process are available, and
\par
we wish to know the contribution of the external data for improving the management process.
\par
Suppose a researcher has the ability to observe the effect of an intensive burn at another site
\par
prior to decision making for the management unit in question. We first calculated the
\par
Expected Value of Perfect Information (EVPI; Johnson and Williams [44]) (see Appendix),
\par
and then calculated EVSI for each combination of scrub state and probability of the null mode
\par
according to Eq (9). Some authors (e.g., Walters [34], Moore and McCarthy [45]) have
\par
observed that EVPI is often low in practice, which is the case in our scrub-jay example (Fig 3).
\par
Expressed as a percentage gain in expected objective value, the value of eliminating model
\par
uncertainty is always {$<$}1\%. This can actually be good news for a manager, in that there is little
\par
incentive to eliminate model uncertainty; a management policy based on an average model
\par
may be sufficient. As expected, values of EVPI are considerably higher than those of EVSI, and
\par
are at a maximum in the interior of the model state. EVPI is uniformly higher for tall-mix,
