\textbf{Discussion}
\par
There is a long record of advances in understanding the processes influencing resource
\par
dynamics, in modeling resource behaviors, in the recognition of resource patterns, and in
\par
methodologies for resource monitoring and estimation. On the other hand, decision making,
\par
including a framework for valuation, continues to lag behind natural resources science, despite
\par
the growth in operations research and decision science (Schwartz et al. [48]). A technical
\par
framework is needed for the evaluation of costs and consequences of resource decisions, so as
\par
to allow a comparative assessment of alternative strategies. With such a framework it then
\par
becomes possible to assess the limitations of uncertainty on decision making, and the value of
\par
eliminating that uncertainty.
\par
PLOS ONE \textbar{} https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0199326 June 29, 2018 11 / 16Value of sample information in structurally uncertain resource systems
\par
In this paper we offer an assessment framework for strategy valuation that builds on adap-
\par
tive management and the value of information. The general goal is to facilitate the assessment
\par
of monitoring in the decision making process, through the consideration of additional value
\par
accruing to additional sampling information. The expected value of sample information serves
\par
as a metric by which managers can explicitly compare the benefit of extended data collection
\par
against associated opportunity and other costs, thereby facilitating smart decision making
\par
based on the efficiency of the additional effort. Advances have been made in recent years in
\par
the value of information with one-time decision making. In this paper we expand on that
\par
work, to address the relatively common occurrence in natural resources of sequential decision
\par
making and monitoring over an extended time frame.
\par
In the above treatment of internal and external monitoring we focused on the marginal
\par
value of external data collection, on the assumption that it could supplement an ongoing pro-
\par
cess of internal monitoring. It should be noted that an analogous assessment is possible,
\par
whereby external investigation is ongoing and it is internal monitoring that is considered to be
\par
supplemental to it. Framing the issue in this way would allow managers to consider whether to
\par
implement (or continue) internal monitoring as part of the management process based on the
\par
marginal value of doing so, or to rely on externally collected data only.
\par
As to the cadence of monitoring, we note that it is possible to extend the period between
\par
monitoring events so that monitoring occurs less frequently than every other year. Consider
\par
the prospect of triennial monitoring, in which a monitoring effort is mounted every 3 years.
\par
A computing form for valuation would mirror that shown above, except it would need to
\par
account for state transitions over 3 years. Again, the valuations for annual and triennial moni-
\par
toring would be equivalent for years in which monitoring occurs, but would differ in years
\par
when there is no monitoring. However, there would be different valuations for the non-moni-
\par
toring years, leading to a differential value-added for annual monitoring that would depend on
\par
the out-year under consideration.
\par
When using EVSI to explore the value of additional information to resolve uncertainty, it is
\par
important not to misinterpret results (Johnson et al. [49]). One such misinterpretation is to
\par
conclude that a low value of EVSI means monitoring is unneeded. As indicated above, EVSI is
\par
a comparison of an average of optimal values produced with additional sample information,
\par
versus the optimal value that is attainable in the absence of additional information (Eq 10). As
\par
such it is effectively a marginal analysis, addressing the value of additional monitoring that
\par
contributes to an ongoing if imperfect monitoring effort that informs decision making. Moni-
\par
toring is required for the state-based information on which the optimal resource decision
\par
making depends, and the question here is whether additional monitoring is justified by the
\par
potential increase in value that would be produced. A decision to increase or decrease the
\par
monitoring effort relies on the answer to this question. Whether to terminate monitoring alto-
\par
gether is a quite different question, one that is not addressed by examining the effect of a mar-
\par
ginal change in monitoring effort (Williams and Johnson [24]).
\par
Finally, we emphasize that as potentially useful as the value of information is, and in particular
\par
EVSI, these metrics only partially characterize the benefit to be derived from the decision frame-
\par
work presented above. Management objectives, potential actions, sources of uncertainty, and fore-
\par
casts of resource responses provide a decision making ``architecture'' for post-decision monitoring
\par
and assessment that can track resource responses and evaluate progress toward objectives. A tech-
\par
nical assessment of the value of the information produced can certainly contribute in informing
\par
management. However, the metrics are certainly not the only, and possibly not even the most
\par
relevant, measures of value for the decision framework. Among other things, a systematic and
\par
structured accounting of the elements of decision making can facilitate collaboration and shared
\par
decision making, lowering the potential for contentiousness and conflict among stakeholders
\par
PLOS ONE \textbar{} https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0199326 June 29, 2018 12 / 16Value of sample information in structurally uncertain resource systems
\par
(Nichols et. al [50]). The value of information can certainly contribute to, but should not obscure,
\par
these and other benefits accruing to a structured process of decision making.
\par
