\par
INTRODUCTION
\par
Ulcerative colitis (UC) is a chronic inflammatory disease
\par
characterized by relapsing and remitting mucosal inflamma-
\par
tion in the colon. The incidence of UC is highest in developed
\par
regions and varies from 17.4 per 100,000 people in Australia to
\par
24.3 per 100,000 people in Northern Europe.1 Due to work-re-
\par
lated opportunity costs in the affected population, the annual
\par
costs associated with UC are reported to be as high as US\$8.1--
\par
\$14.9 billion in the USA and \texteuro 12.5--\texteuro 29.1 billion in Europe.2
\par
Because UC is one of the most common forms of idiopathic
\par
inflammatory bowel disease (IBD), the global burden of UC is
\par
rising in newly industrialized countries, such as China, due to
\par
the rapid economic development.3 The main symptoms of UC
\par
are bloody diarrhoa, abdominal pain, and fatigue, and the goal
\par
of UC management is clinical remission. Aminosalicylates
\par
(5-ASA), immunosuppressants, and biological drugs are
\par
widely administered to patients with UC. However, approxi-
\par
mately 15\% of patients receive a colectomy due to the failure
\par
of these medicines.
\par
Over the last decade, anti-tumour necrosis factor-{$\alpha$} (anti-
\par
TNF{$\alpha$})-based treatments, including adalimumab, golimumab,
\par
and infliximab, have been approved for the management of
\par
active moderate-to-severe UC, which has led to a statistically
\par
significant improvement in clinical response, clinical remission,
\par
and mucosal healing compared to the outcomes of the stand-
\par
ard treatment.4--7 Recently, tofacitinib, which is an oral Janus
\par
kinase inhibitor (JAKi), and vedolizumab, which blocks the
\par
2291
\par
Downloaded from https://academic.oup.com/ibdjournal/article/24/11/2291/4989472 by guest on 05 May 2024Wu et al
\par
Inflamm Bowel Dis {$\bullet$} Volume 24, Number 11, November 2018
\par
gut-homing {$\alpha$}4{$\beta$}7 integrin, have been shown to have potential
\par
for the treatment of moderate-to-severe UC.8, 9 However, the
\par
use of these new medications might increase the already heavy
\par
financial burden on the public. Several recent economic eval-
\par
uations have shown that a first-line anti-TNF{$\alpha$} agent, such as
\par
infliximab, might be a cost-effective option.10--12 However, it is
\par
unclear whether the promising tofacitinib, vedolizumab, and
\par
anti-TNF{$\alpha$} biological treatments and treatment sequences
\par
combining these agents would be cost-effective for patients
\par
with UC. One recent systematic review showed the transferabil-
\par
ity and generalizability of conclusions from the cost-effective-
\par
ness of biologics for UC is limited because the cost inputs are
\par
region-specific.13 The current study investigated the economic
\par
outcomes of introducing tofacitinib, adalimumab, vedol-
\par
izumab, golimumab, and infliximab and their different treat-
\par
ment sequences to the present standard care of patients with
\par
moderate-to-severe UC in the UK and China for the extent of
\par
transferability and generalizability, which are the representa-
\par
tives of high-income and middle-income regions, respectively.
\par
METHODS
\par
Overview
\par
Patients with moderate-to-severe UC who have expe-
\par
rienced failure of at least one prior conventional therapy are
\par
usually managed with conventional therapy and may receive
\par
biological and tofacitinib treatments. The following 6 potential
\par
medicines were considered in the current study:1 conventional
\par
nonbiological therapy (comprising a mix of 5-ASAs, immu-
\par
nosuppressants, and corticosteroids);2 300 mg vedolizumab;3
\par
10/5 mg tofacitinib;4 5 mg/kg infliximab;5 200/50/50 mg goli-
\par
mumab; and6 160/80/40 mg adalimumab. Our models explored
\par
14 competing treatment sequences for moderate-to-severe UC,
\par
which are illustrated in Figure 1. In sequence 1, which served
\par
as the reference control strategy, the patients received conven-
\par
tional therapy without biological therapy. In sequences 2--6,
\par
vedolizumab, tofacitinib, infliximab, golimumab, and adali-
\par
mumab were administered separately. In sequences 7--10, ved-
\par
olizumab, infliximab, golimumab, and adalimumab were first
\par
administered separately, and the patients were switched to
\par
tofacitinib when treatment failure occurred using these med-
\par
icines. In sequences 10--14, tofacitinib was first administered,
\par
and when tofacitinib failed, the patients were switched to ved-
\par
olizumab, infliximab, golimumab, and adalimumab separately.
\par
When the potential biological therapies and tofacitinib failed in
\par
sequences 2--14, conventional therapy was used until death or
\par
surgery occurred.
\par
A Markov-type cohort model was used for the economic
\par
evaluation, and the time horizon of each treatment was divided
\par
into the following 2 periods: period 1 (treatment induction) and
\par
period 2 (treatment maintenance). The cycle lengths of peri-
\par
ods 1 and 2 were 8 and 26 weeks, respectively.14 The baseline
\par
FIGURE 1. 2292
\par
Treatment sequence and schematic of the model for patients with UC.
\par
Downloaded from https://academic.oup.com/ibdjournal/article/24/11/2291/4989472 by guest on 05 May 2024Inflamm Bowel Dis {$\bullet$} Volume 24, Number 11, November 2018
\par
characteristics of the patients were derived from the literature,
\par
their ages are assumed to be 40 years, and their body weights are
\par
assumed to be 77 kg in the UK14 and 62 kg in China.15 Patients
\par
were entered into the model during the induction phase of the
\par
first treatment in the treatment sequence. At the end of induc-
\par
tion phase, the responders (patients who had a clinical response
\par
or remission) continued the current treatment until treatment
\par
failure occurred. A Mayo score was used to evaluate the out-
\par
come of the UC management. Clinical remission was defined
\par
as a total Mayo score {$\leq$} 2 points with no individual subscores {$>$}
\par
1 point. Clinical response was defined as a reduction from the
\par
baseline total Mayo score {$\geq$} 3 points and {$\geq$} 30\% accompanied
\par
by either a rectal bleeding subscore of 0 or 1 or a reduction
\par
from baseline in rectal bleeding {$\geq$}1.1 Patients who did not have a
\par
response or remission during the induction phase and patients
\par
who experienced treatment failure during the maintenance
\par
phase discontinued the treatment, transitioned to an active dis-
\par
ease state, and either began a new treatment in the sequence or
\par
received a colectomy (Fig. 1).
\par
The analysis was conducted from the perspective of the
\par
UK National Health Service (NHS) and Chinese health care.
\par
The costs, quality-adjusted life-years (QALYs) and duration
\par
of clinical remission over a lifetime horizon were estimated for
\par
each comparator. The costs were adjusted to 2013/2014 values.
\par
The annually discounted rates of the costs and outcomes were
\par
3.5\% in the UK and 5\% in China.16 Institutional review board
\par
approval was waived because this analysis was an economic
\par
study based on publicly available data.
\par
Clinical Data
\par
The clinical response and remission rates were used
\par
to estimate the transition matrices in the model, in which
\par
the gained efficacies at week 8 were employed in the ther-
\par
apy induction, and the maintained efficacies at week 26 were
\par
employed in the therapy maintenance. Due to the lack of
\par
matched data, indirect comparisons were performed using
\par
network meta-analyses based on pivotal placebo-controlled
\par
clinical trials investigating 5 active agents,4--9 including the
\par
OCTAVE trial, which investigated tofacitinib; GEMINI 1,
\par
which investigated vedolizumab; PURSUIT, which investi-
\par
gated golimumab; ULTRA 1 and 2, which investigated adali-
\par
mumab; and ACT 1 and 2, which investigated infliximab. All
\par
patients involved in these trials also received background ther-
\par
apy. Because the target population in the current analysis was
\par
not previously exposed to biological agents, the results from
\par
anti-TNF therapy-naive patients were extracted for the net-
\par
work meta-analyses; the Bayesian approach with a fixed-ef-
\par
fect model was applied, and the placebo arms were used as a
\par
reference.17--19 The weighted transition probabilities of conven-
\par
tional therapy from 5 trials and the estimated risk ratio (RR)
\par
for the 5 active agents versus conventional therapy from the
\par
meta-analysis are shown in Table 1 and Appendix Figure 1.
\par
Due to the lack of long-term follow-up data reported from the
\par
Economic evaluation for ulcerative colitis
\par
trials, the analysis assumed that the transition probabilities of
\par
the subsequent maintenance cycles were indefinitely equal to
\par
the inputs of maintenance cycles 1--2 that were obtained from
\par
the maintenance phase of the clinical studies as previously
\par
described.14
\par
The patients with active moderate-to-severe UC require
\par
surgery, and the colectomy probability was 0.0154 (range:
\par
0.0051--0.0258), which was derived from the published liter-
\par
ature.14, 20 The probability of surgery complications also was
\par
extracted from the available data.21--23 We assumed that the com-
\par
plications were resolved within 26 weeks and that the patients
\par
were in postsurgical remission.
\par
Natural mortality could occur in the patients at any point
\par
during the disease course. Because there was no evidence indi-
\par
cating that patients with UC had a lower life expectancy, the life
\par
expectancy tables for the UK and China from the life tables of
\par
the World Health Organization (WHO) member states (2011)
\par
were used in the analysis.
\par
Quality-of-Life Estimates
\par
A systematic search was performed in the PubMed,
\par
Embase, and Web of Science databases to identify utility val-
\par
ues related to UC. The base-case utility values of remission,
\par
response, and active disease were derived from a study reported
\par
by Woehl et al that involved 180 patients with UC in the UK.24
\par
The utility values of postsurgery remission were 0.60.11 The
\par
disutility of surgical complications was 0.17,14 which resulted
\par
in a 0.43 utility score for the states of surgical complications.
\par
Other reported utility values were used in the sensitivity anal-
\par
yses.11, 14, 20, 23 All values are summarized in Table 1. Due to the
\par
lack of Chinese-specific utility data, it was assumed that there
\par
is no difference between the Chinese and UK estimates of UC
\par
utilities.
\par
Costs
\par
The cost estimates used in the analysis, which are pre-
\par
sented as British pounds (\pounds ), are shown in Table 2 and described
\par
below. The following direct medical costs were considered in the
\par
model: treatment initiation and maintenance of conventional
\par
therapy, tofacitinib, adalimumab, vedolizumab, golimumab,
\par
and infliximab and their dispensing, follow-up and monitor-
\par
ing, hospitalization, surgery, and treatment of complications
\par
after surgery. Based on the clinical reports,4--9 the following
\par
administration schedule was used to estimate the drug costs:1
\par
vedolizumab, 300 mg IV at week 0 and 2 and every 8 weeks
\par
thereafter;2 tofacitinib, 10 mg twice daily in week 0--8 and
\par
5 mg twice daily thereafter;3 infliximab, 5 mg/kg IV at weeks
\par
0, 2, and 6 and every 8 weeks thereafter;4 golimumab, 200 mg
\par
SC at week 0, followed by 100 mg at week 2 and then 100 mg
\par
every 4 weeks; and5 adalimumab, 160 mg SC at week 0, fol-
\par
lowed by 80 mg at week 2, and then 40 mg every other week.
\par
A bottom-up (``microcosting'') approach was used to estimate
\par
costs other than treatment medications.25 The health resource
