\par
Sensitivity Analyses
\par
To accommodate the uncertainty of the model inputs
\par
and to test the robustness of the model output, a probabilistic
\par
sensitivity analysis (PSA) and 1-way sensitivity analyses were
\par
conducted. In the PSA, 10,000 Monte Carlo simulations were
\par
performed by inputting values drawn from their statistical dis-
\par
tributions (ie, gamma distribution for costs, normal distribution
\par
for log RRs, and health resource utilization, and beta distribu-
\par
tion for utilities, probabilities, and proportions). A cost-effec-
\par
tiveness curve representing the uncertainty in the model was
\par
plotted to show the probability of the cost-effective simulations
\par
at various willingness-to-pay thresholds. One-way sensitivity
\par
analyses test the corresponding incremental cost-effectiveness
\par
ratios (ICERs) by varying each input parameter over a plausi-
\par
ble range as shown in Tables 1,2, which were mainly obtained
\par
from reported 95\% confidence intervals or assuming 0.25 or 0.5
\par
times the base-case estimate. The analysis was performed using
