RESULTS
\par
Model Validation
\par
The predicted 5-year cumulative colectomy rates with
\par
conventional therapy in the present study were approximately
\par
13\% and were covered by the observational data, which ranged
\par
from 7.5\% to 24\%.32, 33
\par
Base-case Analysis
\par
The results of the base- case analysis of a typical adult
\par
patient with moderate-to-severe UC in the UK and China are
\par
summarized in Table 3. The analyses showed a higher remis-
\par
sion time and QALY gained for sequences containing ved-
\par
olizumab and tofacitinib compared to the outcomes of the
\par
other sequences. Sequence 11 (Tofacitinib {$\rightarrow$} Vedolizumab
\par
{$\rightarrow$} Conventional therapy) was more effective than the other
\par
sequences, with an incremental QALYs that ranges from a
\par
minimum of 0.01 compared with sequence 7 to a maximum
\par
of 1.88 compared with sequence 1 in the UK and incremental
\par
QALYs of 0.01 compared with sequence 7 to 1.80 compared
\par
with sequence 1 in China (Table 3).
\par
The analysis also showed a cost-effective frontier consist-
\par
ing of sequences 1, 6, 10, 14, and 11 in the UK and 1, 3, 14, and
\par
11 in China (Fig. 2). The ICERs of sequences 6, 10, 14, and 11
\par
over sequence 1 were \pounds{} 8,438, 17,397, 17,716, and 26,574/QALY
\par
in the UK, respectively (Table 3). In China, the ICERs of
\par
sequences 3, 14, and 11 over sequence 1 were \pounds 16,707, 22,189,
\par
and 40,436/QALY, respectively (Table 3).
