include this outcome because this risk becomes similar to that
\par
in the general population over time. Fourth, due to the lack of
\par
efficacy data beyond 52 weeks, the transition probability during
\par
the firstf-year maintenance phase was assumed to be constant
\par
and applied indefinitely to the subsequent cycles as performed
\par
in previous reports.11, 14 The long-term health outcomes of these
\par
therapies should be explored in future studies. Finally, we did
\par
not evaluate other potential treatment sequences, such as a
\par
sequence in which vedolizumab is used as a first-line treatment,
\par
tofacitinib is used as a secondline treatment, and anti-TNF{$\alpha$}
\par
2301Wu et al
\par
Inflamm Bowel Dis {$\bullet$} Volume 24, Number 11, November 2018
\par
therapy is used as a third-line treatment, because of the lack of
\par
solid clinical evidence to support these sequences.
\par
CONCLUSION
\par
Our study suggests that improvements in health outcomes
\par
with tofacitinib and vedolizumab were greater on average than
\par
those with anti-TNF{$\alpha$} in patients with active moderate-to-se-
\par
vere UC. The treatment sequences comprising tofacitinib and
\par
vedolizumab were favorable cost-effective options from the
\par
high-income UK NHS perspective, and the tofacitinib treat-
\par
ment was the most cost-effective option from the middle-income
\par
Chinese perspective. These findings could be helpful for better
\par
allocating the limited health resources in treating UC patients.
\par
However, our findings indicate that more studies are required to
\par
evaluate the long-term effects of the anti-TNF{$\alpha$}, anti-Ami, and
\par
JAKi therapies on the efficacy and safety in patients with UC
\par
and the economic and epidemiological impacts of UC using
\par
more solid data regarding costs, quality of life, and treatment
\par
compliance.38
