Abstract
\par
Successful eradication of invasives is facilitated by early detection and prompt onset of con-
\par
trol. However, realizing or verifying that a colonization has occurred is difficult for cryptic spe-
\par
cies especially at low population densities. Responding to the capture or unconfirmed
\par
sighting of a cryptic invasive species, and the associated effort to determine if it indicates an
\par
incipient (small, localized) population or merely a lone colonizer, is costly and cannot con-
\par
tinue indefinitely. However, insufficient detection effort risks erroneously concluding the
\par
species is not present, allowing the population to increase in size and expand its range. Evi-
\par
dence for an incipient population requires detection of 1 individual; its absence, on the
\par
other hand, must be inferred probabilistically. We use an actual rapid response incident and
\par
species-specific detection estimates tied to a known density to calculate the amount of effort
\par
(with non-sequential detections) necessary to assert, with a pre-defined confidence, that
\par
invasive brown treesnakes are absent from the search area under a wide range of hypotheti-
\par
cal population densities. We illustrate that the amount of effort necessary to declare that a
\par
species is absent is substantial and increases with decreased individual detection probabil-
\par
ity, decreased density, and increased level of desired confidence about its absence. Such
\par
survey investment would be justified where the cost savings due to early detection are large.
\par
Our Poisson-based model application will allow managers to make informed decisions
\par
about how long to continue detection efforts, should no additional detections occur, and sug-
\par
gests that effort to do so is significantly higher than previously thought. While our model
\par
application informs how long to search to infer absence of an incipient population of brown
\par
treesnakes, the approach is sufficiently general to apply to other invasive species if density-
\par
dependent detection estimates are known or reliable surrogate estimates are available.
\par
PLOS ONE \textbar{} https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0204302 September 27, 2018 1 / 13Inferring the absence of an incipient population
\par
\textbf{Introduction}
\par
Invasive species can have dramatic ecosystem impacts and result in invasion debts [1], both of
\par
which incur high societal costs [2]. Removal of all individuals of a potentially invasive species may
\par
be possible if detection and control are initiated soon after its introduction [3--6]. Early detection
\par
and rapid response (EDRR) programs for potentially invasive species commonly consist of sys-
\par
tematic attempts to detect incipient populations, followed by eradication or containment efforts if
\par
detected [7]. Typical EDRR practice is to survey defined areas for species presence via one or
\par
more visits to the location until the target species is detected or until searches cease, once there are
\par
repeated failures to detect the target species. Ideally, intensity of the response should be dictated
\par
by the degree of certainty of species absence chosen by resource managers, but in practice avail-
\par
ability of funding and personnel usually dictate the intensity of response. Managers are often
\par
unable to make optimal decisions because they lack the means for determining when their search
\par
efforts have achieved their selected level of certainty.
\par
Many rapid response efforts rely on intensive initial searches of short duration after detec-
\par
tion of an invasive species. Sometimes these efforts are followed by periodic searches over lon-
\par
ger time periods (e.g., [3, 8]) to try and detect the species of concern. For instance, Hodgkins
\par
and Foster [4] reported on the accidental introduction of Italian wall lizards, Podarcis siculus,
\par
in a shipment of stone to the UK from Italy. The initial rapid response captured 4 lizards in a
\par
two-day effort. Afterward, the imported stone shipment and surrounding area were placed
\par
under monthly surveillance for two years with no additional lizard detections. Decisions on
\par
effort duration were not discussed in the paper and presumably were based on expert opinion
\par
combined with a good-faith effort. Such incidents emphasize the need to develop analytical
\par
and decision making tools for EDRR [7].
\par
Containment, suppression, and local eradication of invasive brown treesnakes (Boigairre-
\par
gularis) on Guam are top management priorities because of the ecological damage that has
\par
occurred on the island, and the prospect of similar damage occurring if the snake were to be
\par
accidentally transported to other islands. The brown treesnake Rapid Response Team (BTS
\par
RRT, coordinated by the U.S. Geological Survey) has been deployed {$>$}23 times since 2002 in
\par
response to credible snake sightings or captures on snake-free islands in the western Pacific
\par
Ocean. These search efforts have never resulted in confirmation of any incipient population,
\par
but have always raised the question of when search efforts should cease.
\par
On 03 September 2014 the BTS RRT deployed to the snake-free Pacific island of Rota in
\par
response to an immature brown treesnake capture in a mouse-baited early detection snake
\par
trap by the seaport [9]. Was the captured snake merely a lone stowaway from Guam, or possi-
\par
bly the tip of a hard-to-detect iceberg? No population was discovered, but the question of
\par
when to stop the search arose. While this survey was still ongoing, we developed an objective
\par
means for determining the amount of search effort required to meet resource managers'
\par
desired degree of certainty that a snake population was not present in the search area. Here we
\par
use the Rota EDRR data to populate a model inferring the absence of any incipient population
\par
during a rapid response. Our framework is based on a Poisson distribution with novel inputs
\par
of species- and density-specific detection probability estimates for each detection method
\par
(visual search and trapping), along with the projected or final level of survey effort.
\par
\textbf{Materials and method}
\par
Model parameters and assumptions
\par
The probability of detecting an incipient population is largely dependent on intensity of search
\par
effort and the population density at sites being searched. Our model assumes that there is
\par
PLOS ONE \textbar{} https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0204302 September 27, 2018 2 / 13Inferring the absence of an incipient population
\par
indeed a snake population present in the search area, and quantifies the probability that we
\par
would then find at least one snake, given (1) the effort expended, and (2) the snake population
\par
density at the sites where we conduct searches. For highly invasive species this probability
\par
should be as high as possible, ideally 95\%.
\par
We model the probability of finding 1 snake(s) based on three parameters: the potential
\par
snake population density in the surveyed area, level of effort, and detection probability esti-
\par
mates (for individual snakes) associated with the capture methods (visual search and trapping)
\par
used. The latter estimates are based on data from a fully censused population of marked and
\par
released snakes [10, 11]. In this paper, we apply our model to the visual search effort on Rota
\par
with similar calculations for the trapping effort provided online (see S1 Table and S1 Text).
\par
Application data
\par
The brown treesnake is a notorious invasive species that was accidentally introduced to the U.
\par
S. territory of Guam shortly after World War II. From a founder population of 10 snakes,
\par
and possibly just one gravid female [12], it spread across the island within a few decades [13].
\par
This slender, secretive, nocturnal, and largely arboreal snake is present in all habitats on
\par
Guam, from forests to grasslands. It can reach an adult snout-vent length of 2.3 m, but is more
\par
commonly {$<$}1.3 m. The snake has caused the extinction of most native forest birds, numerous
\par
power outages, and severe cases of envenomation among young children [13, 14].
\par
Guam is a transportation hub of the Western Pacific and there is risk of the snake spreading
\par
and causing similar damage elsewhere. In the past two decades, numerous brown treesnake
\par
captures and credible sightings have occurred on other islands in the Pacific and Indian
\par
Oceans, in North America, and as far away as Spain; all are believed to be snakes originating as
\par
stowaways in aircraft, ships, and cargo from Guam [15]. To minimize the risk to locales with
\par
transportation links to Guam, the United States spends several million dollars annually to
\par
inspect outbound cargo from Guam and remove stowaway snakes [15]. Snake interdiction
\par
programs also exist in Hawai'i and the Commonwealth of the Northern Mariana Islands,
\par
which are on the `receiving' end of the transportation network.
\par
In 2002, the BTS RRT was created to respond to snake sightings/captures on snake-free
\par
islands in the region, to help prevent establishment of brown treesnake populations [15]. The
\par
BTS RRT uses an incident command structure when responding to credible snake sightings
\par
(or captures) and conducts visual searches and trapping. Led by the U.S. Geological Survey,
\par
the BTS RRT conducts annual trainings on and off Guam, maintains communication net-
\par
works with agency partners and at-risk islands, conducts public outreach, and refines detec-
\par
tion tools and deployment capabilities.
\par
Visual search detection estimate and assumption
\par
Detection estimates were based on data from a censused population of marked snakes in a
\par
5-ha snake enclosure on Andersen Air Force Base, Guam. The snake barrier prevents immi-
\par
gration and emigration of snakes from the forested site [11, 16]. The censused snake popula-
\par
tion has provided an opportunity to evaluate the effectiveness of control and detection tools
\par
including visual searching [10] and trapping [11], and to estimate the tool-specific detection
\par
probabilities for individual snakes under well-defined survey efforts along parallel transects at
\par
8-m spacing. Over the past decade, research on Guam has shown that visual searching is an
\par
effective search tool for snakes of all sizes but detection probability does vary by size [10].
\par
In this modeling exercise, we used a visual detection probability estimate (0.07) based on an
\par
average-sized snake (ca. 900 mm) of average body condition found under typical search condi-
\par
tions of no moonlight and low wind [10] and averaged over heterogeneous forest habitats.
\par
PLOS ONE \textbar{} https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0204302 September 27, 2018 3 / 13Inferring the absence of an incipient population
\par
Visual detection estimates are directly related to search effort. In Guam, individual snake
\par
detection probability estimates on a given night were based on 5.94 km of searched transects
\par
(13.5 transects/night \texttimes 0.22 km/transect \texttimes 2 searchers/transect, each looking only to one side).
\par
We define this visual search unit from the Guam snake enclosure as 1 detection effort unit
\par
(DEU). Alternatively, if walking paces were similar to those used during detection estimation,
\par
one could specify 1 DEU in units of time (13.5 transects/night \texttimes 30 minutes/transect \texttimes 2
\par
searchers/transect = 810 minutes or 13.5 hours). Because searchers tend to adjust their pace to
\par
the complexity of habitat (e.g., Guam 0.44 km/h vs. Rota 0.76 km/h) we felt that transect dis-
\par
tance searched would be a better measure of effort for this system. Had Christy et al. [10]
\par
searched less intensively on a given night in the snake enclosure on Guam, fewer snakes would
\par
have been found and the individual-based detection probability estimate(s) would have been
\par
lower. Because we obtained almost identical estimates nearly 1.5 years later in this area when
\par
corrected for effort but after substantial forest regeneration [17] and higher snake densities, we
\par
feel that the detection estimate per visual search effort unit (\textasciicircum{}
\par
p= 0.07) is robust.
\par
We have limited data on snake detection as a function of its distance from a search transect,
\par
and that distance varies with vegetation structure. Applying visual detection rates derived
\par
from Guam to detection efforts on another island, therefore, relies on several assumptions.
\par
The fact that detection probabilities on Guam average 0.07 per night implies that snakes were
\par
rarely detected more than once per standardized visual search effort unit (i.e., 1 DEU), and
\par
detection of any particular individual was a binomial process rather than a count distribution
\par
process involving re-captures (i.e., should marked-and-released snakes have been captured
\par
more than once in a nightly 5.94-km survey effort). This allows extrapolation of detection
\par
probability from an intensive search of a dense population in a small area, to a less intense (i.e.,
\par
geographically more spread-out) search effort in a modest-sized area where there may be an
\par
incipient snake population. This assumes that we confine searching to within the limits of an
\par
incipient population's range and hypothesize the population density of snakes in that area.
\par
We first assumed that the Rota visual search effort was conducted in an area where the
\par
snake population density was the same as in the snake enclosure (117 snakes in 5 ha, or ca. 24
\par
snakes/ha) on Guam during the study of Christy et al. [10]. If so, then one person walking 5.94
\par
km (1 DEU) and searching one side of a transect would likely find (0.07 \texttimes 117) = 8.19 snakes.
\par
We then decreased snake density (linear effect), increased search efforts (linear effect), and cal-
\par
culated a matrix of expected snake detections (Table 1).
\par
Poisson distribution application
\par
Because the goal was to find evidence suggestive of an incipient population, this was the press-
\par
ing question: What is the probability of finding at least one snake, given a specified search
\par
