\textbf{Discussion}
\par
Successful early detection and rapid response efforts for incipient invasive populations require
\par
infrastructure, proven detection/control tools, readiness to respond, immediate access to ade-
\par
quate funding [21] and analytical and decision tools [7], ideally combined with digital data col-
\par
lection [22]. Of these components, analytical and decision support tools have historically
\par
received the least attention. Indeed, prior to our modeling effort, there had been no quantifica-
\par
tion of brown treesnake rapid response efficacy or duration despite numerous sightings in the
\par
Pacific region [15] of this highly invasive species. Over the past decade there has been an
\par
increase in applied analytical and decision support tools for EDRR across taxa that include
\par
optimizing surveillance programs [23--28] and related questions of deciding when to declare a
\par
successful eradication of an established invasive species [25, 29--34], generating model-based
\par
forecasts of invasions [35, 36], and sampling to detect rare species [37, 38].
\par
In our modeling framework we assessed the probability that additional snakes will be found
\par
during surveys, should there be a snake population present in the focal area. We developed
\par
this model based on effort-linked detection probabilities from a censused brown treesnake
\par
population on Guam and calculated the probability that the finite rapid response effort would
\par
have revealed any snake. We adjusted detection probabilities from Guam downward to
\par
account for Rota's more prey-rich environment and then estimated the probability of encoun-
\par
tering any snake(s) under a wide range of hypothetical population densities. Unlike past rapid
\par
response efforts for this species, we can now conduct an analysis grounded in rigorous ecologi-
\par
cal research in order to provide resource managers with the desired level of confidence for a
\par
given deployment effort. We found that had the brown treesnake population density in the
\par
surveyed area been 0.25/ha the deployment should have detected one or more snakes with a
\par
probability exceeding 99\% given the search effort of 639.4 km and an individual snake detec-
\par
tion probability of 0.035 per DEU. Our matrices readily calculate probabilities for inferring an
\par
absence given varying levels of search effort over a range of individual snake detection proba-
\par
bilities (see S1 Table). While we have estimated the probability of inferring the absence of an
\par
incipient population, we acknowledge that it is the decision of regulatory agencies and associ-
\par
ated natural resource managers to determine the level of certainty and what is the reasonable
\par
level of doubt. This decision is a tradeoff between (1) declaring that no incipient population
\par
exists with the risk that the species is present and can continue to grow and spread and (2) con-
\par
tinuing to survey with the risk that the species is not established and limited resources are
\par
wasted. Poisson detection probability matrices clearly show, as one would expect, the slim
\par
probability of detecting average-sized snakes when snake density is low and that the effort to
\par
declare the absence of a species requires a substantial investment of time and resources. Based
\par
on our findings, the minimum sampling effort for inferring an absence may be greater than
\par
PLOS ONE \textbar{} https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0204302 September 27, 2018 8 / 13Inferring the absence of an incipient population
\par
previously estimated for cryptic species [39, 40] or rare species [37] and clearly underscores
\par
the need to increase detectability of such species when feasible.
\par
As with all models there are assumptions. In our framework we used detection estimates
\par
for our case study species from a known population on Guam in which the detection probabil-
\par
ity of a snake of average size, sex, and body condition under modal environmental conditions
\par
and across varied microhabitats was p= 0.07, but snake size and other variables are known to
\par
cause individual variation [10]. Do we need to be concerned about such individual variation
\par
when predicting our likelihood of finding evidence for an incipient population? Yes and no.
\par
Ultimately, we think ``no'' because in our framework, we are not looking at how easy it would
\par
be to removethe lastsnake (which should be the hardest one to detect) of a hypothetical snake
\par
population. We are merely asking if our search effort is sufficient to detect any snake. With
\par
variation around average detection probability, some snakes would be easier to find, some
\par
would be harder to find, and many will be found with the average probability. The question
\par
then becomes this: Is the detection probability effect linear, so that the easier-to-find individu-
\par
als make up perfectly for the harder-to-find individuals, causing the average detection that we
\par
use in our modeling to render an unbiased answer? For this question, we think so. But the
\par
answer to our original question might also be ``yes'' if the detectability varies among individuals
\par
with particular traits, and if the distribution of those traits are different in an incipient popula-
\par
tion compared to the population where the detection probability was estimated. We adjusted
\par
for an assumed systematic population bias in snake satiation by simply multiplying the
\par
assumed detection probability in Rota by 0.5. Similar adjustments might be desirable if traits
\par
such as sex, size, body condition, etc., affect detection probability, and if those traits are
\par
assumed to differ between established and incipient populations due to factors such as habitat
\par
variability. Hence the variation itself is not an issue, but a difference in the population means
\par
might be problematic. Adjusting detection probabilities for hypothetical population traits is
\par
likely to present a challenge but we err on the side of conservatism and present confidence esti-
\par
mates over a range of detection probabilities.
\par
In our case study we lacked specific estimates for searching in low snake density situations
\par
and had to make assumptions. The detection estimates from Christy et al. [10] and Tyrrell
\par
et al. [11] come from dense snake populations. A rarity of targets would likely diminish
\par
searcher efficacy due to a lower positive reinforcement rate [41], although experienced search-
\par
ers such as those participating in BTS RRT deployments are presumably more likely to main-
\par
tain search effectiveness in the absence of frequent rewards. MacKenzie et al. [42] advocate
\par
that information on detectability be `borrowed' or inferred from other times, locations, or
\par
even species; therefore, we think it is reasonable to extend our model to other species when
\par
surrogate density-specific estimates are available. When detection probabilities cannot be rea-
\par
sonably borrowed from the literature, managers could use other options: (1) expert opinion
\par
could be solicited to define a range of plausible detection estimates [43--45], or (2) crude detec-
\par
tion information on a focal taxon could be collected in ways less labor intensive than were
\par
obtained for our focal species by Christy et al. [10]. For the latter option, proxies for the focal
\par
taxon could be placed at random but known locations for searchers to find [46, 47] or an area
\par
occupied by telemetered individuals could be surveyed by ``blind'' searchers [48]. Such work
\par
could be conducted in areas where the focal taxon is already established, rather than in the
\par
area where an incipient population is suspected. We recognize that species-specific estimates
\par
are most critical for species that are highly invasive but hard to detect, and therefore have a
\par
high potential to spread to other sensitive areas; snakes therefore present a very suitable test
\par
case [5, 49] for development of our analytical approach.
\par
Our analysis relies on the assumption that we are searching in the correct area, i.e., where
\par
an incipient population would be expected (here, searching the vicinity around a credible
\par
PLOS ONE \textbar{} https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0204302 September 27, 2018 9 / 13Inferring the absence of an incipient population
\par
sighting). Inferences are limited to the search area, although brown treesnakes are habitat gen-
\par
eralists and virtually all areas on recipient islands consist of suitable snake habitat. As long as
\par
the organism is likely to be distributed across the search area, and somewhat mobile, the size
\par
of the area or how transects are laid out does not matter---only the effort matters. We are
\par
equally likely to run into any snake if we concentrate our searching to a 1-ha area, as if we
\par
were to search one-tenth as intensely over a 10-ha area. A larger area means a lower individual
\par
detection probability, but this is offset by the higher number of targets in a larger area.
\par
For intensive surveys in a small area, the probability of detecting one particularsnake that
\par
currently happens to be present is high, but there are likely to be few available targets. Indeed,
\par
the snake density may be so low that there is probably no snake at all in the survey area on any
\par
given night, although random snake movements across the landscape would eventually cause
\par
them to pass through the survey area. The cumulative probability of detecting any snake is
\par
therefore the same, should we spread our effort over a larger area. This obviously applies to
\par
organisms that are somewhat mobile, and where repeated surveying of the same transects over
\par
time is not a duplicative and wasted effort.
\par
Whereas our model is meant to assess if an incipient population is established in a larger
\par
area, a rapid response elicited by a recent sighting at a well-defined location might argue for
\par
centering the search area within that locale. All searched transects in the 2014 Rota EDRR
\par
effort were within a 47-ha area surrounding the Rota seaport (where a snake had been cap-
\par
tured). We surveyed 639.4 km, rendering a 95\% chance of detecting a snake population of 0.16
\par
individuals/ha. Hence our 95\% probability corresponds to no more than (0.16 snakes/ha \texttimes 47
\par
ha) = 7.5 snakes being present in the search area on an average night. Given a fixed search
\par
effort and a specific confidence level, the larger the search area (over which the effort is
\par
diluted) the more snake individuals could have gone undetected. That is simply because for a
\par
particular organism density, Nscales linearly to area. If responding to multiple sightings of an
\par
invasive species over a larger spatial extent, then a different sampling strategy (e.g., stratified
\par
random sampling [50] or adaptive sampling [51]) would be warranted. Our model should
\par
apply to different sampling approaches.
\par
Cost-benefit [23] and population demography extensions to our modeling framework
\par
could be incorporated (if known) and would further increase the utility of our analytical tool.
\par
For instance, if cost is estimated for one unit of detection effort (DEU), then managers work-
\par
ing within a specified budget could predict costs associated with various levels of certainty.
\par
Using our case study with a hypothetical monetary estimate of US\$100/DEU, we could then
\par
state the following: snake density would need to be at or lower than 0.16 snakes/ha or else the
\par
visual searches (639.4 km) would have turned up at least one snake with 95\% probability and
\par
such an effort would cost US\$10,746 ((639.4 km/(5.94 km/DEU)) \texttimes (US\$100/DEU)).
\par
Finally, if one has prior knowledge of the intrinsic rate of growth in an incipient population
\par
of the focal species, and one were to make assumptions of the current population density and
\par
demography, one could envision modeling the growth of a suspected, but yet unverified, popu-
\par
lation. This might allow estimation of the (higher) probability of detecting any population dur-
\par
ing a repeated survey at some time in the future---which would come at a cheaper labor effort,
\par
but occur when the potentially invasive organism has gained an even stronger foothold in its
\par
new range.
