\par
Monitoring is a key component of risk management at geologic carbon storage (GCS) sites, serving both to help
\par
operators understand and manage site performance, and to assure the public and other stakeholders that ef-
\par
fective containment is maintained and impacts avoided. Potential leakage of CO2 and/or brine through well-
\par
bores, faults, and fractures to potable groundwater resources is a primary risk concern at onshore GCS sites. In
\par
this paper, we present an adaptive methodology for leakage risk-based monitoring design. The methodology uses
\par
a risk event tree to predict the likelihood of leakage occurrence, with detection probabilities of risk events
\par
estimated for multiple monitoring plans. The overall detection probability of a proposed monitoring plan in-
\par
corporates baseline data, stochastically simulated leakage events, and the likelihood that a set of technologies
\par
will detect the changes in baseline conditions induced by the simulated leakage events. The adaptive monitoring
\par
design methodology is demonstrated with a representative case study of CO2 and brine leaking from a well to a
\par
potable groundwater aquifer using simulated data at the High Plains aquifer in the United States. Groundwater
\par
quality parameters, pH, total dissolved solids and benzene concentrations, were used to calculate the corre-
\par
sponding detection probabilities of conventional groundwater sampling and xed sensor monitoring for selected
\par
leakage scenarios. The overall detection probability considering all monitoring information was then calculated
\par
to evaluate proposed monitoring plan designs. Finally, a simple optimization problem to maximize detection
\par
probability with constrained monitoring resources was presented as an application example to close the loop of
\par
monitoring and modeling.
\par
1. Introduction
\par
Geologic carbon storage (GCS)-- wherein large volumes of anthro-
\par
pogenic CO2 are captured from industrial and power generation
\par
sources, and stored in geological formations-- is one of several tech-
\par
nically viable climate-change mitigation strategies (IPCC, 2005). Sta-
\par
keholders, however, including the general public, have expressed con-
\par
cerns about potential environmental impacts associated with large-scale
\par
implementation of GCS. Chief among these concerns is that leakage of
\par
CO2 and displaced brine through natural (e.g., faults and fractures) and
\par
engineered (e.g., wells) leakage pathways could result in contamination
\par
of potable groundwater sources at onshore GCS sites.
\par
Monitoring is an important part of GCS and is included in the risk
\par
assessment, management and communication frameworks re-
\par
commended by several government agencies and authoritative organi-
\par
zations (IEAGHG, 2009; Harbert et al., 2016). Monitoring and mitiga-
\par
tion actions are essential to con rming site performance (Pawar et al.,
\par
2015), and in practice GCS monitoring plans must be able to demon-
\par
strate that the GCS activities meet the regulation requirements
\par
(Hovorka, 2012). Several types of monitoring frameworks have been
\par
proposed to address these management needs in the past decade. Seto
\par
and McRae (2011) proposed a model-based framework for integrated
\par
monitoring design for risk reduction at GCS sites, formulating the
\par
monitoring design problem in a systematic approach in order to con-
\par
sider many factors, such as operational decisions, footprint size, and
\par
uncertainty in monitoring strategies. Yang et al. (2012) proposed a
\par
general framework of Bayesian belief network (BBN) for CO2 leak de-
\par
tection at GCS sites, using leakage simulations and baseline data, and
\par
the BBN framework was illustrated using simulations and measure-
\par
ments from the ZERO Emissions Research Technology (ZERT) study
\par
site. The recent Quest project also used a storage management frame-
\par
work, which includes a measurement, monitoring and veri cation
\par
(MMV) plan to assess the storage risks and evaluate the monitoring
\par
performance in an adaptive manner to ensure conformance and
