\par
2.1.1. Baseline data and threshold determination
\par
The proposed methodology accommodates consideration of
\par
thresholds that serve di erent purposes related to the detectability of a
\par
potentially unwanted change in the engineered natural system, and the
\par
acceptability of that potential change relative to some established
\par
standard (e.g., protective regulatory threshold or engineering perfor-
\par
mance threshold). Detectability thresholds, typically based on back-
\par
ground data, are essential for leakage detection since they allow com-
\par
parison with leakage-induced monitoring signal perturbation to make
\par
inference about leakage occurrence. Previously, a probabilistic method
\par
(Yang et al., 2011) was used to determine the likelihood of a leak based
\par
on the detection probability that a measured monitoring signal is above
\par
a critical threshold value, {$\beta$}par-tech, given a background signal distribu-
\par
tion, Bpar-tech, of a monitoring parameter and a monitoring technology
\par
(together denoted as the par-tech). The threshold values, {$\beta$}par-tech, can be
\par
obtained from the background distribution, Bpar-tech, using prediction
\par
intervals (Yang et al., 2011) or tolerance intervals (Last et al., 2013) in
\par
order to nd the anomalies indicating leakage. In terms of acceptability,
\par
threshold values may also be derived from governing regulations, such
\par
as the U.S. Environmental Protection Agency (EPA) maximum con-
\par
taminant levels (MCLs) (U.S. EPA, 2016) or California MCLs (California
\par
EPA, 2016), which are intended to protect the environment and public
\par
health. Engineering performance thresholds are involved with natural
\par
128Y.-M. Yang et al. or practical measurement constraints, such as method detection limit or
\par
operational range of a speci c technology for a monitoring parameter.
\par
Unlike the xed regulatory-type thresholds, the background data-based
\par
thresholds may be spatially or temporally varying (i.e., {$\beta$}par-tech(x,y,z,t),
\par
if signi cant spatial or temporal patterns are shown). For example, if
\par
spatial data are su cient, the 2D distribution mapping based on
\par
background measurements can be made using kriging simulations.
\par
Moreover, if a monitoring parameter depends on other variables (e.g.,
\par
temperature or redox conditions), its background distributions and the
\par
data-based thresholds can subsequently be explained and modeled as a
\par
function of these variables.
\par
