4. Discussion
\par
In addition to threshold selection, the variability of the background
\par
data and the magnitude of leakage-induced change are important fac-
\par
tors a ecting the detectability of a monitoring parameter (Osenberg
\par
et al., 1994; Macmillan and Creelman, 2005). If the background
\par
variability of a monitoring parameter is unacceptably large, it could
\par
mask leakage-induced signals of interest, leading to low detection
\par
sensitivity. In some cases, a portion of this background variability may
\par
be attributed to underlying phenomena that can be modeled by un-
\par
derlying explanatory variables, and e ectively improve detectability.
\par
Another important factor is the relative magnitude of leakage-induced
\par
change of a monitoring parameter, which is used to select a responsive
\par
monitoring parameter. The magnitude of leakage-induced change can
\par
be estimated by leakage simulation and modeling of monitoring re-
\par
sponse, eld experiments, or a combination thereof.
\par
Calculating the probability of detection at given monitoring loca-
\par
tions considers the two factors (background variability and observed
\par
signal magnitude), and this monitoring location-centered detection
\par
probability can be easily updated at the operational stages (using real
\par
eld observations) as well as be used at the planning stage (using si-
\par
mulated monitoring responses). The use of background distribution to
\par
estimate the PD allows the calculation of false positive and false ne-
\par
gative rates, and thus enables application in probabilistic risk assess-
\par
ment methods, including not only the event tree discussed here, but
\par
also simulation-based Bayesian belief networks, site performance utility
\par
functions, etc. This is the primary advantage over the more conven-
\par
tional single threshold method, which estimates detection probability
\par
using zero (below threshold) or one (above threshold) without use of
\par
the background distributions.
\par
No matter what monitoring technologies are used, both baseline
\par
data and modeling of monitoring responses (i.e., forward modeling of
\par
monitoring response based on the results of transport process modeling)
\par
are essential to demonstrate the CO2 and brine plume conformance and
\par
containment e ectiveness, particularly the agreement between the re-
\par
servoir or system model predictions and the monitoring observations
\par
(Chadwick and Noy, 2015). Disagreement between the predicted and
\par
the observed plume extent does not necessarily indicate increased risk
\par
