\par
of leakage or a hazardous event. It does provide new information about
\par
the geologic system and its response to CO2 injection that can be used as
\par
feedback to adaptive monitoring design and to update risk assessment
\par
and risk management (Shell Canada, 2010). For example, data from a
\par
3D seismic survey can suggest presence of preferential ow paths that
\par
can be incorporated to iteratively improve geologic characterization
\par
used in reservoir simulation; the same information can also be used to
\par
inform decisions about locating new monitoring wells.
\par
In addition to the parameter uncertainty re ected by the stochastic
\par
leakage simulations and the model scenario uncertainty of di erent
\par
monitoring technologies, there are other sources of uncertainty in the
\par
proposed framework. For the inputs from site information and risk
\par
identi cation as shown in Fig. 2, scenario uncertainty exists, not only in
\par
the simulations of possible geologic risk events and monitoring pro-
\par
cesses, but also in the identi cation of potential risk events through
\par
expert knowledge and judgment. During a GCS project, our knowledge
\par
of potential risk scenarios increases, from roughly knowing what events
\par
could happen to better understanding of detailed processes of how
\par
these events happen. This type of uncertainty, which is due to lack of
\par
knowledge, can be reduced through gathering more information and
\par
data, is usually called epistemic uncertainty or subjective scienti c
\par
uncertainty (Morgan et al., 1992; Winkler, 1996; O'Hagan, 2004). Si-
\par
milarly, model uncertainty exists in the models for risk event simula-
\par
tion, the processes that a ect monitoring responses, probability models
\par
used for parameter characterization, as well as various methods for
\par
approximation, including expert elicitation. The model functional
\par
structure and modeling approaches, such as software, code and algo-
\par
rithm selection or numerical implementation, would be adjusted and
\par
re ned, and thus the overall model uncertainty is usually reduced
\par
through the project life cycle. One noted study in GCS model un-
\par
certainty is the Sim-SEQ project (Mukhopadhyay et al., 2015), which
\par
attempted to understand and quantify model uncertainties for geologic
\par
carbon sequestration through an international model comparison.
\par
Moreover, the parameter uncertainty addressed via the stochastic si-
\par
mulations in our case study is usually regarded as aleatory uncertainty,
\par
which exists in most data due to random variability and measurement
\par
errors. (For more details about the analysis of this stochastic simulation
\par
data, see the publications by Carroll et al. (2016) and Mansoor et al.
\par
(2014) and the supporting information).
\par
Simulation models for geophysical processes, similar to many en-
\par
vironmental systems models, require a hierarchical set of judgments
\par
and analyses to characterize variability (most often re ective of alea-
\par
tory uncertainty) and uncertainty (most often epistemic). For example,
\par
site data are collected and stochastic models developed from these data
\par
to characterize the spatial distribution of soil and rock properties such
\par
as porosity and permeability. Even if the distribution type and its
\par
parameters are known for di erent strata at a site (e.g., a lognormal
\par
distribution with its parameters, along with the spatial correlation
\par
function from a variogram or a similar model), the spatial distribution
\par
of porosity and permeability would still be subject to aleatory un-
\par
certainty due to di erences in the large number of possible random
\par
elds that are consistent with the stochastic model, conditioned on a
\par
limited number of imperfect measurements at the site. However, this
\par
random aleatory uncertainty is just the rst tier of the hierarchy. The
\par
underlying model structure and parameters that describe the spatial
\par
variability of porosity and permeability are themselves uncertain. This
\par
scienti c uncertainty is epistemic in nature, and it can be reduced with
\par
improved science and models.
\par
As the operations at a site evolve over time, the collection of more
\par
site data will serve to reduce the aleatory uncertainty of the spatial
\par
distribution of model parameters, for example, porosity and perme-
\par
ability, since these data will further constrain the particular realization
\par
of these properties that is present at the site. These same data and the
\par
associated model building, testing and updating that ensue also help to
\par
reduce scienti c (epistemic) uncertainty in appropriate process and
\par
statistical models that support porosity and permeability estimation,
\par
both at the site in question and for others. The assessment and simu-
\par
lation approaches proposed here are modeled after this hierarchical
\par
approach to uncertainty, learning, and adaptive response, enabled by
\par
closing the loop of monitoring and modeling.
\par
This type of aleatory uncertainty in parameters has been commonly
\par
seen in the recent GCS leakage simulations (Celia and Nordbotten,
\par
2009; Kopp et al., 2010; Siirila-Woodburn et al., 2017; Sun et al.,
\par
2013b; Dai et al., 2014; Carroll et al., 2014), including NRAP's in-
\par
tegrated assessment models (Pawar et al., 2016). Despite the im-
\par
portance of parameter uncertainty of simulations, a broader check of all
\par
possible scenarios of potential risk events and the detectability of these
\par
events is necessary in practice from the perspective of risk management.
\par
That is what this methodology framework seeks to provide, using a risk
\par
event tree to address the scenario uncertainty from the source and the
\par
monitoring detection probabilities of risk events to include the un-
\par
certainties in the monitoring process at the eld site. Of course, para-
\par
meters also have epistemic uncertainty since our knowledge about
\par
parameters is increasing through the project, and this epistemic un-
\par
certainty in parameters can be quanti ed using Bayesian inference
\par
(Morgan et al., 1992; O'Hagan, 2004), which facilitates the adaptive
\par
138Y.-M. Yang et al. part of the monitoring and modeling loop. For a detailed review of
\par
potential sources of uncertainty in the proposed framework, please see
\par
the supporting information.
\par
Developing full physics models that consider the full range of geo-
\par
logic parameter data, model, and scenario uncertainty (including un-
\par
certainty in leakage pathway types and potential leakage locations)
\par
would be extremely computationally expensive, to the point of being
\par
impractical. Therefore, several strategies can be applied to reduce this
\par
computational burden, such as prioritizing the model parameters to
\par
consider only those that are most important; performing focused si-
\par
mulations at the locations most representative of important geological
\par
properties, with greatest leakage likelihood, or presenting important
\par
safety or environmental concerns; or simplifying complicated geologic
\par
structure, physical and/or chemical interactions with reduced physics
\par
models or reducing computational cost with reduced order models. Our
\par
strategy is to use a set of focused and representative stochastic simu-
\par
lations of a single wellbore leakage event, and then to preserve the si-
\par
mulation uncertainties in summarized low, median and high mon-
\par
itoring response scenarios. Next we convert the monitoring response
\par
results into probabilities of detection (PD) and perform the general-
\par
ization step at the PD level. Through working on the PD results where
\par
the values are between 0 and 1, the issue of ne-tuning full physics
\par
models could be less concerned (e.g., simulated pH value 5.1 and 5.6 all
\par
have the same PD value 1). Given the framework of IAMs, this gen-
\par
eralization step can be regarded as a reduced order model of monitoring
\par
responses in terms of leakage detectability.
\par
In practice, the feedback to monitoring design could incorporate
\par
information from sources other than physics-based systems simulations
\par
and site monitoring. The information feedback loop can also take into
\par
account information that can come from expert judgment (e.g., using
\par
expert judgment to assess prior probabilities of leakage pathways and
\par
locations to populate the event tree, as shown in the owchart), and
\par
other factors (e.g., cost, labor requirements, regulatory constraints, land
\par
accessibility, and receptor vulnerability). The initial monitoring design,
\par
then, provides a framework into which new information collected in the
\par
rst time step can be synthesized, and used to update the physical
\par
systems simulations, the monitoring models and risk assessments in
\par
subsequent time step. This, in turn, provides the basis for comparison
\par
with new monitoring information used in adaptive monitoring design in
\par
the next time step. On a higher level, the system of monitoring-mod-
\par
eling feedback can be treated as a systems dynamics model by in-
\par
corporating utility models for decision making, such as the cost of
\par
leakage impact consequences or the bene t of successful storage
\par
(Oldenburg et al., 2011; Bielicki et al., 2014; Trainor-Guitton et al.,
\par
2013).
\par
Besides the detection probability for maximum coverage, other
\par
variables can also be used as evaluation objectives, such as the
\par
minimum time for detection (Gastelum and Porter, 2014; Yonkofski
\par
et al., 2016; Nowak et al., 2015, 2015; Bode et al., 2016), minimum
\par
operational cost (Nowak al., 2015; 2015; Bode et al., 2016), or
\par
minimum environmental impacts or leakage volume (Sun et al., 2013a).
\par
Multiple evaluation criteria may also be considered together using
\par
multi-objective optimization methods (Sun et al., 2013a; Nowak et al.,
\par
2015), including utility functions (Bode et al., 2016). When multiple
\par
decision criteria are considered, and the site speci c geospatial data are
\par
su cient to support spatially varying simulations, optimization of
\par
plume monitoring problems can become more complicated and com-
\par
putationally intensive. Given this complexity, some geo-statistical
\par
methodologies can be applied and appropriate optimization algorithms
\par
can be studied (De Gruijter et al., 2006; Dhar and Datta, 2007; Prakash
\par
and Datta, 2013; Helle and Pebesma, 2015).
\par
Methodologies such as that presented herein o er a clear, objective
\par
framework for quantitative assessment of the e ectiveness of networks
\par
of monitoring technologies applied over a large spatial domain. It al-
\par
lows both the ability to update monitoring design in response to new
\par
observations of site response to storage, and assessment of system
\par
International Journal of Greenhouse Gas Control 76 (2018) 125--141
\par
concordance to predicted behavior (Oldenburg, 2018), and allows
\par
quantitative assessment of the relative e ectiveness of di erent mon-
\par
itoring scenarios. Site-speci c, quantitative, risk based approaches
\par
present a more defensible means of considering tradeo s between
\par
monitoring design robustness and frugality than qualitative, heuristics-
\par
based approaches. More work is needed, however, to develop and va-
\par
lidate such comprehensive site-scale monitoring design and evaluation
\par
structures and work ows.
\par
5. Conclusions
\par
A risk-based monitoring assessment methodology is proposed for an
\par
adaptive monitoring design considering leakage risk, focused on in-
\par
tegrating the risk assessment process into monitoring design work ow
\par
and providing a framework to close the loop of monitoring and mod-
\par
eling. To demonstrate how this risk-based monitoring assessment assists
\par
the monitoring design, a simple wellbore leakage risk event tree for
\par
known and unknown wellbore leakage was built and demonstrated
\par
using a set of stochastic groundwater wellbore leakage simulations re-
\par
presentative of the High Plains aquifer. The probabilities of detection of
\par
proposed monitoring designs for known and unknown wellbore leakage
\par
were estimated by incorporating background information, detection
\par
thresholds and simulation of the monitoring parameters and techniques
\par
selected, given the summarized low, median and high monitoring re-
\par
sponse scenarios. The estimated detection probabilities were linked to a
\par
probabilistic leakage risk event tree to complete the overall detection
\par
probability estimation structure for the monitoring design.
\par
Additionally, to evaluate the general detectability for selected mon-
\par
itoring parameters, monitoring techniques and selected thresholds,
\par
monitoring spatial and temporal resolution analyses were performed
\par
using detection probability for random leaks. With the High Plains
\par
aquifer leakage simulations, groundwater pH is the dominant mon-
\par
itoring parameter in leakage detection, as compared with TDS and
\par
benzene concentrations, given selected monitoring techniques. A com-
\par
bined probability of detection was calculated to incorporate results
\par
from all monitoring parameters to bene t leakage detection diagnosis.
\par
Finally, to illustrate the adaptive monitoring design optimization pro-
\par
cess, a simple adaptive optimization problem of maximum detection
\par
probability for a hypothetical simulated impact domain with feedback
\par
through two time steps was presented as a simple application example
\par
to close the loop of monitoring and modeling.
\par
The application of this risk-based monitoring assessment metho-
\par
dology can be expanded in many aspects. In addition to groundwater
\par
monitoring shown here, this assessment methodology can be applied to
\par
any monitoring parameters provided that monitoring responses can be
\par
modeled as a function of simulated leakage event. The probabilistic
\par
design of this methodology accommodates full risk assessment in-
\par
cluding calculation of false positive and negative rates and allows the
\par
integration of various utility models (e.g., environmental impact, op-
\par
erational cost, etc.) to provide more comprehensive decision support.
\par
Moreover, other leakage detection diagnosis techniques, such as ma-
\par
chine learning methods, can be used rather than the basic signal and
\par
detection threshold distribution approach presented here. Estimation of
\par
leakage detection probability can be considered with real monitoring
\par
data to evaluate CO2 plume conformance. Instead of the simple adap-
\par
tive optimization example (single-objective over two time steps), multi-
\par
criteria decision analysis and optimization with utility models can also
\par
be performed, considering more decision variables with a more com-
\par
plicated feedback time schedule. Besides wellbore leakage, additional
\par
leakage pathways can be considered, such as fault and cap rock, and
\par
site speci c expert survey of prior leakage pathway probability can be
\par
performed to accommodate more comprehensive risk analysis. The
\par
adaptive nature of this methodology allows for implementation of
\par
Bayesian methods in leakage detection. The resolution and complexity
\par
of monitoring models can be easily tuned to match the system scope
\par
and resolution needed to support decision making. Additionally, the
\par
139Y.-M. Yang et al. International Journal of Greenhouse Gas Control 76 (2018) 125--141
\par
classic event tree concept could be easily extended to incorporate other
\par
types of risk and liability management problems, such as geologic
\par
carbon storage with brine displacement for pressure management and
\par
plume control.
\par
Future work will focus on implementing this risk-based metho-
\par
dology into NRAP's IAM framework to allow feedback to monitoring
\par
network design and risk management decision support. More compre-
\par
hensive system modeling including geologic carbon storage system with
\par
more monitorable parameters/intervals and more detailed leakage
\par
pathway representations will be tested using other real and synthetic
\par
site data. Finally, the full risk assessment including false positive and
\par
negative rates for more detailed monitoring design and the addition of
\par
utility functions in terms of human and environmental aspects (in ad-
\par
dition to operation cost) will be explored. Going forward, development
\par
of a multi-criteria decision analysis tool linked to integrated assessment
\par
model framework and application of this methodology to actual eld
\par
data will also be pursued.
