\textbf{Abstract }This paper considers a closed-loop supply chain consisting of one-
\par
manufacturer and one-retailer. This supply chain provides single-kind products with
\par
reusable containers. The main purpose of this study is to explore and evaluate the value
\par
of recovery information captured by embedded sensors in the environment of internet
\par
of things. The recovery information of containers dynamically monitors recovery sta-
\par
tus and provides a reliable estimation of return quantity. The value of information is
\par
measured by the cost saving performances with full, partial or no recovery informa-
\par
tion. When the full or partial recovery information is available, the decisions are made
\par
based on the known quantities of the usable or total return flow. When no recovery
\par
information is available, the decisions are made based on the stationary distribution
\par
of the return flow. A periodic inventory model is built with uncertainties of forward
\par
and reverse flows. Then, a myopic order policy is proposed for the different levels
\par
of information utilization. Through the optimality analysis, we introduce a farsighted
\par
inventory control policy. Using the general result of Markov decision processes, the
\par
performance of heuristic policies is displayed. The farsighted policy performs better
\par
than the myopic policy. In addition, the farsighted policy helps to lessen the convex
\par
impact of utilization rate on the expected cost. Afterwards, we extend the model with
