\textbf{1 Introduction}
\par
With increasing environmental awareness and legal requirements, closed-loop sup-
\par
ply chain (CLSC) has received continual attentions by both academics and industrial
\par
practitioners (Kleindorfer et al. 2005). For the managers of CLSC, the inventory man-
\par
agement with returns is complex. This is primarily due to the additional uncertainties
\par
enforced by reverse material flow besides the regular uncertainties of forward flow.
\par
The recovery uncertainty which involves uncertain return time and quantity is the
\par
most common and critical to stochastic inventory control amongst other kinds of
\par
uncertainties. Owing to the uncontrollability and invisibility of recovery in CLSC, the
\par
traditional method to handle recovery uncertainty is estimation based on known para-
\par
meters or historical information. Different from the traditional method, our method
\par
utilizes recovery information from the embedded sensors of reusable containers, which
\par
may lead to extra supply chain benefit.
\par
The reusable container is a durable storage vessel throughout the nodes of sup-
\par
ply chain. In a long time, it has been treated as an important part of CLSC (Kroon
\par
and Vrijens 1995). Traditional reusable containers only protect the main products in
\par
bumpy transportation, such as glass bottles of beverages. Novel perceptible reusable
\par
containers that are triggered by embedded sensor technology, like radio frequency
\par
identification devices (RFID) or internet of things (IoT), can provide other services
\par
(like product tracker) and enhance the recovery value (Visich et al. 2007; Shi et al.
\par
123268 J Comb Optim (2018) 35:266--292
\par
2011). A sensor, once incorporated with the containers, can monitor the position,
\par
storage content, temperature, or other critical information of containers recovery. By
\par
means of wireless communication, central manager will receive these data and per-
\par
ceive the critical events of the sensor embedded containers. Some researchers have
\par
proposed the similar approach. Kiziltoprak et al. (2008) found that increasing sea
\par
freight containers can be managed by smart proactive containers and the correspond-
\par
ing decentralized handling framework. Menesatti et al. (2012) investigated that the
\par
smart container which is supported by a geographic information system (GIS) assures
\par
the production with less waste and more economical advantage. Mason et al. (2012)
\par
gave an example of prototype gas cylinders inventory management system in Liver-
\par
pool. Based on RFID and wireless sensor network technology, the tag of their system
\par
detected its states information and actively transmitted it to the database. Follow-
\par
ing their works, this paper assumes that the central manager can dynamically obtain
\par
recovery information of the end-of-life products and containers. The recovery infor-
\par
mation dynamically monitors recovery status and provides a reliable estimation of
\par
return quantity. The word ``dynamically'' means that the central manager can only
\par
acquire the recovery information in one period of time before recovery or disposal
\par
behavior.
\par
Smart containers and returnable transportation items (RTI) of perishable prod-
\par
ucts are critical to improve efficiency and service quality. In the work of Ketzenberg
\par
et al. (2015), their smart containers recorded the temperature and time information of
\par
perishables for ensuring food to be sold in the order of first-expired first-out. Their
\par
simulation study showed the averaging 43.2 \% cost saving by the dynamic temper-
\par
ature and time information. The containers they used were much more expensive
\par
than ordinary one because of their extra function. Thus, it is commercially bene-
\par
ficial for supply chain to reuse their smart containers instead of buying new ones.
\par
For some products with high environmental hazards like NI-MH batteries, recov-
\par
ery is required by the law and business accountability. If the availability of products
\par
is gone or reduced to some level, the container would be sent to recovery center
\par
by customers especially when a reward of recovery or a punishment of holding
\par
is given. However, the recovery time of containers is influenced by many uncer-
\par
tain factors: such as stocking time, using frequency, and product quality. Therefore,
\par
any kind of estimation method leads to an estimation bias. There is a gap between
\par
powerful visibility of smart containers and the effectiveness of CLSC inventory
\par
management. This gap motivates the technology improvement of embedded sen-
\par
sors.
\par
Besides embedded sensor technology, all supply chain agents need to share data,
\par
information, and corresponding knowledge based on a public resource ontology frame-
\par
work. Learning from Grubic and Fan (2010), we can obtain a systematic review of
\par
several kinds of supply chain ontology models, but they are unsuitable to products
\par
recovery process. Geerts and O'Leary (2014) raised a term of ``supply chain of things''
\par
which represents a highly visible supply chain with IoT technology and EAGLET
\par
(Event, Agent, Location, Equipment, and Thing) ontology model. It is a promising
\par
knowledge sharing framework for a recovery system.
\par
Our goal is to explore the value of information (VOI) which motivates supply chain
\par
to implement a costly sensor embedded container recovery system. Our model can
\par
123J Comb Optim (2018) 35:266--292 269
\par
be applied in many different settings where the reusable containers are used for per-
\par
ishable products (like foods) and durable products (like furniture). In the meanwhile,
\par
the difference of reusable qualities of the containers is also considered in this paper.
\par
The aim of our model is to determine the optimal serviceable inventory level of min-
\par
imum expected cost. The rest of this paper is organized as follows: In Sect. 2, the
\par
previous related works are reviewed. In Sects. 3 and 4, the inventory control model
\par
and the corresponding myopic policy are proposed. In Sect. 5, via the optimality
\par
analysis of myopic policy, we present a more effective farsighted inventory control
\par
policy. Then we extend the model to selective disposal behavior. VOI is analyzed in
\par
Sect. 6 through a simulation study. Finally, Sect. 7 concludes our research with future
\par
work.
\par
\textbf{2 Literature review}
\par
This paper is related to three parts of research. The first part describes the inventory
\par
management method of reusable containers, which use many estimation methods and
\par
develop variable model structures. The second part summarizes recent research about
\par
the management models which apply the embedded sensor into CLSC or other relative
\par
domains. The third part is some enlightening literatures about VOI in CLSC. All parts
\par
are reviewed briefly in the following.
\par
\textbf{2.1 Inventory management of reusable containers}
\par
The reusable containers recovery is a kind of sub-process of remanufacturing. Its pur-
\par
pose is reducing management cost and environmental impact during all life-cycle of
\par
products. Early research is well reviewed by Guide et al. (1999) and Guide (2000).
\par
They pointed out the importance of reliable prediction method about the life-cycle of
\par
products in inventory control. Nakashima et al. (2004) and Li et al. (2009) solved a
\par
stochastic programming model of single-product remanufacturing by directly using
\par
the value iteration method. In addition, Nakashima et al. (2004), Teng et al. (2011),
\par
and Parvini et al. (2014) assumed that a fixed fraction of demands are returned imme-
\par
diately in the models. This assumption is not realistic because the in-use time normally
\par
exceeds one period and the return rate is not determined. With the consideration of
\par
return uncertainty, Clottey et al. (2012) improved the estimation performance when
\par
the return lag is exponential and the delivery lag is within one period. de Brito and
\par
Laan (2009) studied the impact of imperfect information on control policy with the
\par
different level of information sources. The most robust combination they found was the
\par
control policy with historical sales information and return distribution. Kenn\'e et al.
\par
(2012) considered a hybrid manufacturing/remanufacturing planning problem with
\par
random processes of operations. Ahiska and Kurtul (2014) also developed a hybrid
\par
manufacturing/remanufacturing system, while they extended the substitution feature
\par
of products to satisfy the demands of secondary market. Besides the production plan-
\par
ning problem, Vorasayan and Ryan (2006) modeled the products refurbishing process
\par
as a queuing network. They formulated a mathematical program to find the optimal
\par
price and proportion to refurbish. However, they did not analyze the impact of utiliza-
\par
123270 J Comb Optim (2018) 35:266--292
\par
tion rate. Some researchers focused on the assumption of demand-dependent return.
\par
Subrata Mitra (2013) modeled the return flow as a partly positive demand-dependent
\par
stochastic process. As a kind of improvement, Zerhouni et al. (2013) studied the impact
\par
of the dependency between demands and returns. They concluded that the base-stock
\par
policy is optimal when return and demand are independent or return lag is zero. While
\par
they cannot prove that base-stock policy is also optimal when return lag is positive.
\par
Pourakbar et al. (2014) considered a finite-horizon EOL components inventory prob-
\par
lem with phaseout returns. When the time and quantities of phaseout returns were
\par
known, they proved the optimal policy is a time-varying threshold level for repairing
\par
work. Then they highlighted the importance of information due to that the uncertainties
\par
about the phaseout schedule and quantity can impair the performance. Owing to the
\par
complexity of problem, Zolfagharinia et al. (2014) utilized a near-optimal simulation-
\par
based search method to solve a two-echelon inventory problem with non-stationary
\par
stochastic demand and return.
\par
Specific for containers recovery, we should first realize that the biggest difference
\par
between container recovery and product recovery is the position of new purchas-
\par
ing goods. In product recovery new purchasing is the external source of serviceable
\par
inventory, however it is the external source of remanufacturing inventory in container
\par
recovery. The traditional method is also the estimation of return flow like in Toktay
\par
et al. (2000). They built the inventory control model for single-use cameras with the
\par
uncertainty and unobservability of return flows. In their case, an empty camera is a
\par
kind of container which is indispensable for providing service and recoverable for
\par
cycle-use. Through modeling the remanufacturing process as a queuing network, five
\par
kinds of estimation-based control policies were proposed where the data is collected
\par
from camera record information and sales historical information. Chew et al. (2002)
\par
focused on the case of gas cylinders recovery in Singapore with long in-use time and
\par
negligible loss rate. They evaluated the reusable inventory with four performances
\par
to provide some advices about underlying dangers. Atamer et al. (2013) studied the
\par
pricing and production decision of reusable containers. The return quantity of their
\par
model was directly dependent on the customer demand and the acquisition fee with
\par
no consideration of uncertainty. Other two papers (Kim et al. 2014; Kim and Glock
\par
2014) concentrated on the container recovery in CLSC with stochastic return time and
\par
quantity, respectively. The former was built for perishable products which deteriorate
\par
during the delivery delays of containers. The latter introduced RFID technology into
\par
the container recovery system to reduce the uncertainty of recovery. Both of them just
\par
considered the determined demand and the continuous-time model for the economic
\par
quantities of lot size.
\par
In summary, researchers tend to simplify recovery as a less-complex process (pure
\par
stochastic flow or demand-dependent stochastic flow) and ignore the closed-loop mate-
\par
rial flow in the periodic model. This is caused by the uncontrollability and invisibility
\par
of recovery in CLSC before the manager can dynamically obtain the recovery infor-
\par
mation. In this paper, the return flow is dependent on the total in-use product rather
\par
than dependent on the demand. Based on the circular structure of model, this paper is
\par
more persuasive than the others with catenulate structures because it is closer to the
\par
real world.
\par
123J Comb Optim (2018) 35:266--292 271
\par
\textbf{2.2 Models with embedded sensor}
\par
In previous work, many researchers deemed that the area of using embedded sen-
\par
sors to monitor the all-life-cycle of products is promising. The tendency of applying
\par
dynamic information into the management model becomes increasingly apparent in
\par
recent years. Yang et al. (2009) proposed Product Service Systems which actively uti-
\par
lize the product life cycle data from sensors to provide service. Lee and Chan (2009)
\par
studied the location selection problem where the dynamic RFID sensor is suggested
\par
to count the quantities around collection points. They utilized a genetic-based algo-
\par
rithm which can obtain good-quality solutions in the simulation. After describing the
\par
schematic Kanban-based computer disassembly line, Ilgin and Gupta (2011) evalu-
\par
ated the improvement potential of embedded sensors as an approach to cope with
\par
the uncertainty of non-functional or missing component before disassembly work.
\par
Kumar and Chan (2011) proposed a superiority search and optimization algorithm
\par
to solve the embedded closed loop logistics problem where RFID embedded system
\par
easily counts the returned products. Cao et al. (2011) introduced a product lifecy-
\par
cle information system with a real case. The uncertainty of products EOL activities
\par
can be diminished through relative information generated by information technology.
\par
In the works of Ondemir et al. (2012) and Ondemir and Gupta (2014), they uti-
\par
lized IoT and RFID to acquire remaining-life time information of components. Then
\par
they built a large scale integer programming model to provide an optimal dissemble-
\par
to-order and remanufacture-to-order solution. The former one proposed and solved
\par
a single-objective problem which considers the cost saving value of remaining-life
\par
time information. The latter one exploited the goal-programming approach to solve a
\par
multi-objective problem of products recovery. Both of them ignored the uncertainty of
\par
demand and return time. Bryan and Srinivasan (2014) evaluated the value of real-time
\par
information from IoT on the beginning-of-life cycle of products. They assumed that
\par
the products are transported in a supply chain with the several stages of congested
\par
transportation. The impact of real-time information on long-term average cost sav-
\par
ing is significant when the transportation conditions are available to the retailer. The
\par
above research did not consider the situation with multiple uncertainties. This kind of
\par
situation is taken into account in this paper.
\par
\textbf{2.3 VOI in CLSC}
\par
Many researchers paid attention on VOI or CLSC, but not many directly bridged these
\par
fields. Ketzenberg et al. (2006) built a CLSC model with uncertain demands/returns
\par
and analyzed it in five different information conditions where they evaluated the VOI of
\par
cost saving. Their result was extended by Ketzenberg (2009) by adding a disposal deci-
\par
sion and the capacitated limit of production. The VOI of long-term average criterion
\par
was depicted in his work. While the disposal decision in his work had no influence on
\par
return flow because the return flow was a stationary stochastic sequence. Panagiotidou
\par
et al. (2013) focused on sampling and procurement decisions of a stochastic remanu-
\par
facturing system. They found that the yield information can significantly improve the
\par
profit of remanufacturing. Ruiz-Ben\'itez et al. (2014) built a model of products collec-
\par
123272 J Comb Optim (2018) 35:266--292
\par
tion interval managing. Their objective is to minimum the sum costs of transportation
\par
and products decay. They analyzed both static and dynamic cases to evaluate the value
\par
of dynamic return quantity information.
\par
To the best of our knowledge, there is no previous work on the inventory manage-
\par
ment of reusable containers with simultaneous considerations of the uncertain features
\par
of demand, return flow, and reusable container. In the meantime, the potential cost
\par
saving value of real-time recovery information is not explored in pervious papers. In
\par
this paper, recovery information reduces the uncertainties of return flow and reusable
\par
containers. Another contribution is that we propose two heuristic base-stock policies
\par
through cost functions analysis instead of utilizing the iteration result directly.
\par
