\textbf{3.2 Assumptions}
\par
This model is developed based on the following assumptions:
\par
(1) Order cycle time, supply lead-time, and recovery lead-time are all 1 period of
\par
time.
\par
(2) The inventory of containers is in a lost-sales setting. Under this assumption, \emph{o}
\par
is smaller than \emph{x }in every period. The inventory of products allows temporary
\par
negative values, which means the unsatisfied demand will become backorder.
\par
(3) Inventory condition (\emph{x}, \emph{y}) is known in real-time. The new containers order and
\par
products order (\emph{o},\emph{q}) made in period \emph{t }will be sent one lead-time later.
\par
(4) The customer uses the products for an unknown time. During period \emph{t}, there are \emph{Rt}
\par
containers which are useless for the customer. The demand which is just satisfied
\par
will not be returned. This assumption ensures the independence between \emph{dt }and
\par
\emph{rt }. After usage, every recovered container is possible to reuse with the probability
\par
of \emph{u}. Other damaged containers are sent to disposal. Under the full recovery infor-
\par
mation condition, \emph{rt }is known prior to the arrival of usable containers. Under the
\par
partial information condition that the quality information of recovered containers
\par
is unknown, \emph{Rt }is known, but \emph{rt }needs to be estimated. Under the no information
\par
condition, both \emph{rt }and \emph{Rt }need to be estimated.
\par
(5) Reusable containers are as good as new ones which can be put into production
\par
directly.
\par
(6) Demand arrives according to the function of {$\phi$} (\emph{d}) as the probability density
\par
function and {$\psi$} (\emph{d}) as the cumulative density function.
\par
(7) Inventory and demand are bounded.
\par
\textbf{3.3 Analysis using Markov decision processes}
\par
This inventory control problem is modeled as a discrete-time Markov Decision Process
\par
(DTMDP). MDP, which was first developed by Bellman (1957), is useful for a range of
\par
problems under the situation where the payoff is partly based on dynamic environment
\par
and partly based on decisions like the recovery process in CLSC. In Toktay et al.
\par
(2000) and other papers, supply chain was modeled as a continuous-time MDP which
\par
views the processing and distribution as a single desk queuing system. However, if the
\par
processing and distribution need inter-enterprise cooperation, a model with fixed cycle
\par
(like a week or a month) may be more reasonable. Thus, we choose the discrete-time
\par
MDP model to describe this problem. In the work of Nakashima and Gupta (2013),
\par
they divided the inventory system into the actual inventory that is in store and the
\par
virtual inventory that is used by customers. Under this assumption, the CLSC was
\par
modeled as material flow between two types of inventories. This model employs the
\par
123274 J Comb Optim (2018) 35:266--292
\par
similar method to classify inventory systems and proposes the virtual inventory of
\par
products (\emph{V I P}) that is stored by costumers.
\par
The events of one period occur in the following sequence:
\par
(1) The new containers \emph{q}, products \emph{o}, and reusable containers \emph{r }are delivered to the
\par
inventories at first.
\par
(2) The retailer realizes the demand \emph{d}. Then the demand (which includes current
\par
demand and backlogged demand) is satisfied with products in the inventory. The
\par
unsatisfied demand is backordered.
\par
(3) Through checking the inventory condition and next period available recovery
\par
containers, the new containers decision \emph{o }and the production decision \emph{q }are made
\par
by the supply chain manager.
\par
(4) The production process of products is done at the end of period.
\par
The key elements of MDP are space, decision, payoff function, transition probabil-
\par
ity, and criterion. The space in this model is (\emph{x}, \emph{y}, \emph{V I P}). The decision is (\emph{o},\emph{q}). The
\par
payoff function is critical to the inventory management effect. This paper considers
\par
the following costs: the purchase cost of new container \emph{c}, the holding cost of container
\par
\emph{hc}, the holding cost of product \emph{h p }, and the punishment cost of lost-sales \emph{P}. The payoff
\par
function \emph{f }is shown in Eq. (1) where \emph{y}+ = max (\emph{y},0) and \emph{y}-
\par
