\textbf{4 Myopic order policy}
\par
Heuristic policy provides a compromise between performance and computational
\par
demand. In this section, the myopic policy of full, partial, and no recovery infor-
\par
mation is proposed to extend the analysis of VOI. Directly calculating the optimal
\par
policy is impractical since it incurs the well-known ``curse of dimensionality''. We
\par
propose and analyze the myopic order policy based on the optimization of expected
\par
cost functions.
\par
\textbf{4.1 Myopic policy of full recovery information}
\par
In this model, inventory control is viewed as open-network control when full recovery
\par
information is available. In this myopic order policy, order decisions are used for
\par
maintaining two stage inventory levels \emph{So }and \emph{Sq }to satisfy the demands during one and
\par
two periods, respectively. According to the work of Federgruen and Zipkin (1986), the
\par
(\emph{s},\emph{s}) policy is optimal for periodic inventory control due to the K-convexity character.
\par
The next step is to calculate \emph{So }and \emph{Sq}.
\par
The product supply during one period cannot exceed \emph{x }+ \emph{o }due to the lead time
\par
of production. At the same time, the product supply during 2 periods cannot exceed
\par
\emph{x }+ \emph{y }+ \emph{o }+ \emph{r }due to the restriction of container quantities. The cost function of \emph{So}
\par
is shown in Eq. (6) where \emph{CSo }just considers holding cost and punishment cost for
\par
backlogging setting.
