\par
first observation is that \emph{EC}s are convex with \emph{u}. This result is also found by other
\par
researchers. The farsighted policy lessens the convexity of \emph{EC }when comparing with
\par
the myopic policy. This is due to its significant cost saving value when \emph{u }is high.
\par
The difference with \emph{VOPI }and \emph{VOFI }is significant when \emph{u }is low and unobvious
\par
otherwise. The second observation is that VOIs are concave with \emph{u}. This result shows
\par
that the high-visibility inventory control policy performs best when the quality of
\par
containers is sound. This phenomenon is caused by the unnecessary containers input
\par
and the underestimated inventory level which is tardily ``digested'' with extreme high
\par
\emph{u}. The pure pull order policy cannot avoid the inventory bullwhip in CLSC. This result
\par
was also observed by the simulation experiment of Zanoni et al. (2011). However in
\par
the analysis of Zhou and Disney (2006), the automatic control policy can lessen the
\par
inventory bullwhip when return rate is high. An easy-understanding and widely-used
\par
inventory policy is ``one-missing-one-add'' that order one external container when
\par
one in-use container is out-of-service. The ``one-missing-one-add'' policy will not
\par
bring about the bullwhip effect. It is only applicable to the condition of low fluctuant
\par
demands, since the inventory cost is ignored.
