\textbf{6 Simulation study}
\par
There are three purposes for the simulation study of the farsighted policy: the first
\par
one is to extend this research to the long-term average cost saving environment; the
\par
second one is to accommodate the inaccurate information; the third one is to relax the
\par
assumption of exponentially distributed in-use time with uniform distribution. The
\par
discounted cost saving which is shown in numerical example contributes to making
\par
decision about fixed investment like building information system or device purchasing.
\par
The long-term cost is vital for enterprises which already have technology foundation or
\par
large-amount government financial support. If the long-term cost saving of recovery
\par
information exceeds the average maintenance cost, then capturing this information
\par
is worthy to proceed. Because of the defect information processing technology and
\par
the uncertain behaviors of customers, the recovery information assumed in this paper
\par
may be inaccurate. It is critical for the farsighted policy to show robustness in extreme
\par
distorted environment.
\par
Each experiment is simulated for 3100 periods and replicated for 50 times. The
\par
first 100 period are warm-up for demonstrating stationary states. The random stream
\par
of demand throughout the experiment is identical for avoiding experiment error. The
\par
simulation study comprises a set of 3 * 2 * 2 *5 = 60 experiments. With three cases of
\par
information, there are totally 180 experiments to provide 120 results of VOI. The
\par
parameter is shown below.
