low penalty cost (low time-efficient). In the condition of inaccurate utilization rate,
\par
the accurate quality information of reusable containers is important for the products
\par
with the features of high \emph{u}, short mean in-use time, and high penalty cost (all opposite
\par
with inaccurate life information). On the whole, the accurate life information is more
\par
important than the accurate quality information for the farsighted policy.
\par
\textbf{6.3 VOI with uniformly distributed in-use time}
\par
The above discussion is based on the assumption of exponential distributed in-use time.
\par
The uniformly distributed in-use time situation is vital to the realistic performance
\par
of the farsighted policy. To this end, we design a uniformly distributed in-use time
\par
condition of simulation where the in-use time is a stochastic value with 1--7 or 1--
\par
15 discrete uniform distribution. Other parameters and settings are unchanged. The
\par
policies of full and partial information situations are unchanged with the uniformly
\par
distributed in-use time. Some modification is needed for the no information situation
\par
due to the change of the stationary distribution of \emph{r }. The result of VOI is shown in
\par
below Table 4 and this result is presented in a graphical format in Fig. 6 of Appendix.
\par
The VOI with uniformly distributed in-use time changes moderately. Other sensitivity
\par
findings are still observed in this condition. Through this simulation experiment, we
\par
can conclude that the farsighted policy still has the cost saving value when applying
\par
into uniformly distributed in-use time products.
\par
\textbf{7 Conclusion and future study}
\par
Past research regarded return flow as a stochastic external input. However, this paper
\par
highlights the total-life-cycle movement of containers. After reviewing pervious work,
\par
the corresponding inventory control is built as a MDP model. Based on the optimization
\par
of expected cost functions, a heuristic myopic policy is proposed in full, partial, and
\par
123288 J Comb Optim (2018) 35:266--292
\par
no recovery information cases. Through the analysis of the myopic optimality, we
\par
introduce the farsighted policy. We list some conclusions below.
\par
(1) When the myopic policy is applied, the inventory cost may exceed expectation if
\par
return flow is bigger than demand.
\par
(2) The myopic policy is optimal when \emph{r }{$<$} \emph{r }{$\ast$}, where \emph{r }{$\ast$} is a unique value in (0,{$\infty$}).
\par
(3) Considering the whole possibilities of return flow, the farsighted inventory level
\par
is no more than the myopic inventory level.
\par
(4) In the numerical examples, the VOI of discounted cost saving is shown. The
\par
farsighted policy performs better. The heuristic VOI of the farsighted policy is
\par
similar to the optimal VOI. The farsighted policy helps to lessen the convex impact
\par
of \emph{u}. \emph{u }has a concave impact on the VOI and a decreasing impact on the value
\par
difference. Then we extend the model with the selective disposal behavior which
\par
stabilizes the \emph{EC }in different \emph{u}. However, the selective disposal behavior decreases
\par
the VOI when \emph{u }is low.
\par
(5) In the simulation study, the VOI of long-term average cost saving and its sensitiv-
\par
ity are shown. Then, this study extends the model with inaccurate information and
\par
uniformed distributed in-use time. The result shows that the accurate life infor-
\par
mation is more important than the accurate quality information for the farsighted
\par
policy. The memorial character of in-use time has no significant impact on the
\par
VOI.
\par
Some management insight is found in this paper. Firstly, the high visibility of recov-
\par
ery process can save cost by almost 10 \% in discounting criterion and 4 \% in average
\par
criterion. Secondly, Due to the convex impact of \emph{u }on \emph{EC}, the appropriate dependabil-
\par
ity of containers is necessary to choose by the manufacturer. In most time, the perfectly
\par
solid container is not a good choice. Thirdly, the quality of life information is very
\par
critical to the cost saving performance. Thus, more investment is needed for improving
\par
the estimation ability of smart containers. Moreover, the enterprises with embedded
\par
sensor containers should consider some managerial measures about enhancing the
\par
control of container returning (like executing a daily interventional check or provid-
\par
ing an in-time returning bonus). In contrast, the quality of utilization rate information
\par
is less important. Finally, the selective disposal operation has potential to be rewarding
\par
particularly when the utilization rate is high.
\par
Future work can be developed in two directions. The first direction is to enrich
\par
the model with more inventory decisions and necessary restrictions. Besides new
\par
containers ordering and disposal, some behaviors, such as short-term containers rent-
\par
ing (\emph{x }{$<$} 0) and products disassembling (\emph{o }{$<$} 0), are worthy of consideration. The
\par
second direction is the transformation of model. This model can be extended to semi-
\par
MDP which considers the randomness of delivery/production processes. Also it can
\par
be extended to partial-observation-MDP which considers inaccurate information.
