Abstract
\par
Purpose-- Social recommender systems have recently gained increasing popularity. The purpose of this paper
\par
is to investigate the influences of informational factors on purchase intention in social recommender systems.
\par
Design/methodology/approach-- Specifically, this study validated the mediating effect of trust
\par
in recommendations and the perceived value between informational factors and consumers' purchase intention.
\par
Findings-- The results confirm that recommendation persuasiveness was a strong predictor of trust in
\par
recommendations and perceived value. Recommendation completeness was positively related to trust
\par
in recommendations and perceived value as well. Trust in recommendations and perceived value was found
\par
to be strong drivers of purchase intention.
\par
Originality/value-- The author identifies two sets of informational factors, i.e. recommendation
\par
persuasiveness and recommendation completeness, which are relevant to consumer attitudes. The current
\par
study proved that informational factors on consumers' purchase intention are fully mediated through trust in
\par
recommendations and perceived value.
\par
Keywords Purchase intention, Perceived value, Informational factors, Social recommender systems,
\par
Trust in recommendations
\par
Paper type Research paper
\par
1. Introduction
\par
Traditionally, online shopping websites (e.g. Amazon.com) have encouraged customers to
\par
write their reviews related to product quality and product satisfaction to share their
\par
consumption experiences with other customers (Qiu et al., 2012). Such online eWOM has
\par
been identified as a typical source of online consumer recommendation (Wang et al., 2015).
\par
Nevertheless, with the rapid development of social network sites, such as Facebook and
\par
Twitter, social recommender systems have become a fashion trend (Chang and Hsiao, 2013).
\par
Compared to traditional recommender systems, social recommender systems aim to deliver
\par
the most relevant and attractive information to users (Wang et al., 2016). Such personalized
\par
recommendations are based on a user's social relationships or preferences (Li et al., 2013).
\par
Consumption profile similarity, social networks and communication logs are possible
\par
sources of relationship data in social recommender systems (Arazy et al., 2010).
\par
The prior research has proposed various social recommender systems to provide
\par
comprehensive recommendation to consumers; nevertheless, the increasing number of
\par
information may cause the problem of information overload (Chen et al., 2009) and may be
\par
challenging to consumers' limited information processing abilities (Gao et al., 2012).
\par
Consequently, providing persuasive and complete recommendation information to consumers
\par
to help them to make a better purchase appears to be particularly important. In spite of the
\par
promised advantages, critical challenges remain in the provision of social recommender
\par
services to consumers. One ongoing challenge is to enhance consumers' trust and perceived
\par
value in recommendations and to improve consumers' purchase intentions. Perceived value
\par
refers to a consumer's overall evaluation of a product or service according to the ratio of
\par
This paper forms part of the special section on Social recommender systems: impact on individual life
\par
and society.
\par
Online Information Review
\par
