\par
perceived benefits and perceived costs (Lin and Wang, 2006). It is commonly known that not
\par
all recommendations are compulsorily written by real persons, and comment spam frequently
\par
appears on online recommender websites (Filieri et al., 2015). Because of such false information
\par
in recommender systems, trust and perceived value become the main concerns. The questions
\par
are: Why do consumers trust and value recommender information in social recommender
\par
systems? Does trust in recommendations and perceived value affect consumers' purchase
\par
intentions? Although trust and perceived value have proved to be important predictors of
\par
consumers' purchase intentions in the e-commerce context (McCole et al., 2010; Chiu et al.,
\par
2012; Li, Jiang and Wu, 2014), little attention has been paid to trust in recommendations and
\par
the perceived value of recommendations (Hsiao et al., 2010) and whether trust in
\par
recommendations and perceived value affect consumers' purchase intentions in social
\par
recommender systems. To fill this gap, this study attempts to investigate the influences of
\par
informational factors (i.e. recommendation persuasiveness and recommendation
\par
completeness) on purchase intention in social recommender systems. Specifically, we
\par
investigated the mediation effect of trust in recommendations and perceived value between
\par
informational factors and purchase intention.
\par
This study contributes to the prior studies in several ways. First, some previous studies
\par
have mainly concentrated on the technological improvement to enhance the recommendation
\par
accuracy of social recommender systems. This study aims be one of the first to explore the
\par
impact of social recommender systems on consumer decisions from a psychological
\par
perspective. Second, this study extends the elaboration likelihood model (ELM) to the social
\par
recommendation context and sheds light on the impact of the mediation effect of trust in
\par
recommendations and perceived value between informational factors and purchase intention.
\par
2. Theoretical background and hypotheses
\par
2.1 Social recommender systems
\par
Recommender systems can predict items that are unexpected or that may be of interest to
\par
online consumers, thereby boosting the sales and customer satisfaction of e-commerce
\par
websites (Gedikli et al., 2014). Recommender algorithms are generally classified into
\par
content-based filtering (CB), collaborative filtering (CF) and hybrid recommender systems (He
\par
et al., 2016). CB analyses the contents of documents and provides ratings; CF utilizes user's
\par
previous product comments or consumption history and hybrid recommender systems
\par
integrate recommendation techniques to achieve better performance with fewer weaknesses
\par
(Damiani et al., 2015; Gedikli et al., 2014; Park et al., 2012). At present, most recommender
\par
systems are integrated into social network sites, which provide access to users' profiles and
\par
interactions (Yang et al., 2014). Social recommender systems employ additional data in terms
\par
of users' social relationships in filtering relevant information to users (Deng et al., 2014).
\par
Consumption profile similarity, social networks and communication logs have been identified
\par
as possible sources of relationship data in social recommender systems (Arazy et al., 2010).
\par
The analysis of such relationship data has become an additional resource for the improvement
\par
of recommendation performance (Alina Christensen and Schiaffino, 2014).
\par
One existing research stream on social recommender systems focuses on optimization
\par
algorithms that accurately predict user preferences, such as Seo et al.'s (2017) friendship
\par
strength-based personalized recommender system and Capdevila et al.'s (2016)
\par
recommender system for a popular location-based social network. Various algorithms
\par
have been proposed to enhance the function of social recommender systems, such as
\par
utilizing the usefulness of tag and time information in social tagging systems (Zheng and Li,
\par
2011), employing social network information to provide an advantage to recommender
\par
systems (Sun et al., 2015), proposing a social recommender system that improves knowledge
\par
sharing in online forums (Li et al., 2012), proposing a model that integrates the frequency,
\par
duration and recency of social tags, as well as users' social networks (Huang et al., 2014),proposing a social recommender mechanism for location-based group commerce (Li, Chou
\par
and Lin, 2014), and proposing a social network-based service recommendation method with
\par
trust enhancement (Deng et al., 2014). In general, the prior research has focused on
\par
algorithms that emphasize relative improved performance in the provision of precise
\par
recommendations compared with benchmark models.
\par
The second stream examines the impact of recommender systems on consumer
\par
decisions. For example, Hennig-Thurau et al. (2012) put forward a theoretical framework to
\par
examine the effects of group recommenders and empirically tested these effects through two
\par
choice experiments. Some studies have proved that consumer attitudes toward a
\par
recommended product influence consumer purchase intention (e.g. Ku and Tai, 2013). The
\par
perceived usefulness of recommendations and trust was proved to have significant
\par
influence on user attitudes toward and intention to make online purchases (Filieri et al., 2015;
\par
Hsiao et al., 2010; Hsu et al., 2013). The effectiveness of using social interaction factors was
\par
examined to explain personalized recommender systems (Choi et al., 2017).
\par
2.2 Elaboration likelihood model
\par
The ELM proposes a theoretical framework to demonstrate and predict how consumers
\par
process information under different situations (Petty and Cacioppo, 1986). The model
\par
argues that persuasion outcomes are a function of strength of content, peripheral cues
\par
and elaboration likelihood in terms of the extent to which an individual process the
\par
issue-relevant information that is included in persuasive communication.
\par
The ELM indicates that there are two routes to persuasion: the central and peripheral
\par
routes (Cacioppo et al., 1986). The central route is related to the elaborate scrutiny of the
\par
content of the information, thus, the persuasion is determined by the readers' cognition of
\par
the information content. The peripheral route refers to content-irrelevant predictors that are
\par
employed by information readers to evaluate the information, which also have significant
\par
impacts during information processing (Cacioppo et al., 1986; Luo et al., 2013; Zhang, 1996).
\par
In addition, the ELM indicates the degree to which elaboration likelihood can influence or
\par
change attitude formation (Luo et al., 2013). When the elaboration likelihood is high, readers
\par
will spare no effort to assess the quality of the received information. In this situation,
\par
informational factors are inclined to be the critical predictors of information reader attitudes.
\par
However, when the elaboration likelihood is low, information readers will not engage the
\par
cognitive effort that is required to assess the received information content, but they will
\par
instead employ content-irrelevant cues to evaluate the information.
\par
When examining the predictors of online behavior, various prior studies (Yi et al., 2013;
\par
Chang and Wu, 2014; Cheng and Ho, 2015; Bi et al., 2017; Hussain et al., 2017) have employed
\par
the ELM as their theoretical basis. Information quality (central route) has been proved to be
\par
an important predictor of consumer attitudes (Ghasemaghaei and Hassanein, 2015; Kim and
\par
Park, 2012; Nicolaou and McKnight, 2006). Information quality has been found to be
\par
positively related to perceived individual benefit and user satisfaction (Zheng et al., 2013).
\par
Moreover, information quality has been proved to have a positive influence on expected
\par
transaction performance, goodwill trust and competence trust, while it is negatively related
\par
to perceived exchange risk (Nicolaou et al., 2013). It has been suggested that providing
\par
information of high quality is the basis of smart decision making and improved results, and
\par
it is the principal goal of any information system (Petter et al., 2013).
\par
However, the previous studies have usually treated central route factors, i.e., information
\par
quality, as a single variable (Chavez et al., 2015; Chen et al., 2015). In this study, we identify
\par
two specific sets of central route factors, i.e. recommendation persuasiveness and
\par
recommendation completeness, which are relevant to consumer attitudes, i.e. trust in
\par
recommendations and perceived value. In the social recommender system context,
\par
recommendation persuasiveness and recommendation completeness are two important
