aspects of information quality. Recommendation persuasiveness refers to the extent to
\par
which the consumer considers the argument of the recommendation to be persuasive or
\par
valid in supporting its viewpoints (Cheung et al., 2009; Luo et al., 2013). Recommendation
\par
completeness refers to the extent to which the recommendation contains a broad set of full
\par
and accurate information (Luo et al., 2013; Mckinney et al., 2002).
\par
2.3 Hypotheses
\par
Trust involves a willingness to adopt vulnerability related to positive anticipation about the
\par
intentions or behaviors of another party (Choi et al., 2011). Trust is critical in the online
\par
environment because it assists customers in overcoming perceptions of uncertainty and risk
\par
when sharing personal information or making purchasing decisions (Ladhari and Michaud,
\par
2015; McKnight et al., 2002). Having faith and less perceived risk are key elements in the
\par
search for new products or services in an online environment (Hajli, 2015). Trust can
\par
efficiently reduce behavioral hesitation in terms of intentions to buy on e-commerce
\par
websites (Fang et al., 2014; Pavlou, 2003; See-To and Ho, 2014). For the purpose of acquiring
\par
anticipated information about the quality and performance of a product or service,
\par
customers will evaluate whether the recommendations that are provided by a social
\par
recommender system are dependable (Filieri et al., 2015; Hsiao et al., 2010). If the customers
\par
consider the recommendations that are offered by the social recommender system to be
\par
trustworthy, they will be more likely to adopt the recommendations and have greater
\par
purchase intention. In contrast, if the recommendation is perceived to be unauthentic,
\par
customers will decrease their intention to buy to avoid being deceived. Thus, the following
\par
hypothesis is proposed:
\par
H1. Trust in recommendations was positively related to purchase intention.
\par
In this study, we consider perceived value to be utilitarian value, that is, the perceived
\par
utility of a social recommender system. Perceived value involves the ability of a social
\par
recommender system to assist consumers in making better choices. It reflects the
\par
effectiveness of a social recommender system to correctly determine the actual quality
\par
of a product, matching recommended products and filtering out uninteresting ones
\par
(Gedikli et al., 2014).
\par
Previous studies have confirmed that perceived value is related to consumer decision
\par
behavior (Xu and Yao, 2015). When consumers facing a decision-making task, they will
\par
evaluate the received information. If the received information can satisfies their product
\par
seeking demands, they will take these information into account (Shen et al., 2013). In the
\par
social recommender system context, if customers consider that the recommendations that
\par
are offered by a social recommender system are helpful for online shopping, they will
\par
increase their purchase intention. Conversely, if the recommendation is perceived to be
\par
useless, customers will decrease their intention to buy. Thus, the following hypothesis
\par
is proposed:
\par
H2. Perceived value was positively related to purchase intention.
\par
Recommendation persuasiveness involves the degree of a consumer considers the argument
\par
of the recommendation to be persuasive or valid in supporting its viewpoints (Luo et al.,
\par
2013). Prior studies have proved that recommendation persuasiveness will directly impact
\par
consumer attitudes in the online context. If consumers view the argument of a
\par
recommendation to be convincing and valid, they will cultivate a positive attitude toward
\par
the information; conversely, if consumers view the argument of a recommendation to be
\par
unconvincing and invalid, they will form a negative attitude toward the information
\par
(Cheung et al., 2009). In the social recommender system environment, it is believed that
\par
consumers will develop positive attitudes such as trust in recommendations when theyconsider the content of a recommendation to be persuasive. Thus, the following hypothesis
\par
is proposed:
\par
H3a. Recommendation persuasiveness was positively related to trust in recommendations.
\par
Recommendation completeness involves the degree of the recommendation contains full and
\par
accurate information (Luo et al., 2013; Mckinney et al., 2002). Recommendation
\par
persuasiveness has proved to be a critical element that is employed by consumers to
\par
assess incoming information. It has been argued that persuasiveness is strongly relevant to
\par
perceived value and can persuade the consumer to adopt or ignore certain products (Gedikli
\par
et al., 2014). Customers must seek product related or service related information; thus, a
\par
higher degree of recommendation persuasiveness makes it more likely that they will
\par
perceive their information seeking demands to have been satisfied (Xu and Yao, 2015). Thus,
\par
the following hypothesis is proposed:
\par
H3b. Recommendation persuasiveness was positively related to perceived value.
\par
Arguments quality provided by a recommender will determine its degree of influence on the
\par
information receiver (Li et al., 2016). Recommendation information may directly affect
\par
consumer purchase decisions (Hsiao et al., 2010), and risk is an inherent attribute of online
\par
purchasing (Hussain et al., 2017; McKnight et al., 2002). More information can result in better
\par
comprehension of a product and, thus, a better choice (Gao et al., 2012). Thus, the offering of
\par
current, precise, relevant, helpful and complete information can cut down consumers'
\par
perceived risk and, thus, improve consumers' trust in recommendations (Wang and
\par
Benbasat, 2016; Yi et al., 2013). Thus, the following hypothesis is proposed:
\par
H4a. Recommendation completeness was positively related to trust in recommendations.
\par
In the social recommender system context, where recommendation information is the critical
\par
resource that is provided by a system (Choi et al., 2011), if consumers can acquire
\par
comprehensive recommendation information, they will have a better comprehension of the
\par
expected product and, thus, a preferable attitude toward the system. Conversely, if consumers
\par
consider recommendation information to be inaccurate and/or vague, they will not trust the
\par
recommendation and will perceive the recommendation to be inferior and valueless. Thus,
\par
recommendation completeness should play a positively role in effecting consumer attitudes to
\par
a recommendation, which underscores the significance of trust in recommendations and
\par
perceived value. Thus, the following hypothesis is proposed (Figure 1):
\par
H4b. Recommendation completeness was positively related to perceived value.
