\par
able to answer it directly and anonymously. Ultimately, 161 persons fully completed the
\par
questionnaires (Table I).
\par
3.2 Measurement
\par
All of the items were adapted from the prior research to ascertain content validity. Four
\par
items were used to measure recommendation persuasiveness that were adapted from Yee
\par
Cheung et al. (2009) and Luo et al. (2013). Recommendation completeness was measured
\par
using a four item-scale from Luo et al. (2013). Three items were used to measure perceived
\par
value that were adapted from Stefanovic et al. (2016). Three items were used to measure
\par
trust in recommendations and three items that were used to measure purchase intention that
\par
were adapted from Hsiao et al. (2010). A five-point Likert scale that ranged from 1``strongly
\par
disagree'' to 5``strongly agree'' was employed to measure all of the items. The items are
\par
included in the Appendix.
\par
3.3 Common method variance
\par
Harman's one-factor test was employed to examine the common method variance
\par
(Podsakoff et al., 2003). All of the items were entered into an unrotated exploratory
\par
factor analysis. In this study, the first component with the largest eigenvalue explains
\par
43.481 percent of variance, which does not exceed 50 percent of all variance. The results
\par
indicate that common-method bias is not an issue in this study.
