4.1 Measurement model
\par
Following the guideline proposed by Fornell and Larcker (1981), the reliability and validity
\par
were assessed in this study. First, Cronbach's {$\alpha$} was employed to test consistency reliability.
\par
The minimum value of Cronbach's {$\alpha$} was 0.731 while the maximum value was 0.782, thus
\par
exceeding the critical value of 0.7 (see Table II). Then, we examined the measurement model
\par
in terms of convergent validity. The confirmatory factor loadings for all of the items were
\par
greater than the minimum cutoff point of 0.7 (see Table III). As is shown in Table II, the AVE
\par
values ranged from 0.590 to 0.696, which were greater than the recommended value of
\par
0.5, and all of the composite reliability values were above the required acceptable level of 0.6.
\par
We also tested the discriminate validity of the measurement model. As is shown in Table IV,
\par
the maximum correlation of any pair of constructs was 0.703, while the minimum square
\par
root of AVE was 0.773. Thus, the square roots of AVEs for each construct were greater than
\par
all of the inter-construct correlations. In conclusion, the measurement model showed
\par
adequate reliability, convergent and discriminate validity.
