\par
5. Discussion
\par
5.1 Discussion of results
\par
This study validated the impacts of informational factors on purchase intention in
\par
social recommender systems. Specifically, we investigated the mediated role of trust in
\par
recommendations and perceived value. First, we found that information quality related factors,
\par
that is, recommendation persuasiveness and recommendation completeness, have significant
\par
impact on consumer attitudes to a recommendation. Specifically, the results confirm that
\par
recommendation persuasiveness is a strong predictor of trust in recommendations and
\par
perceived value. Consumers do not simply adopt the recommendations blindly. They will select
\par
recommendations contains strong and convincing arguments. Moreover, if a recommendation
\par
cannot convince the consumers, they will be inclined to question its authenticity. Consumers
\par
are more willing to trust and value recommender information that is persuasive.
\par
Moreover, recommendation completeness was positively related to trust in
\par
recommendations and perceived value. These results empirically suggested that it is
\par
important for information content to take consumer attitudes into consideration. When
\par
reading recommendations that were provided by social recommender systems, both
\par
attributes of information quality result in significant effects on consumer attitudes, that is,
\par
trust in recommendations and perceived value. The more recommendation persuasiveness
\par
and recommendation completeness are considered to be trustworthy and valuable, the
\par
stronger its impact on customers' purchasing intentions will be.
\par
Finally, trust in recommendations and perceived value was found to be strong drivers of
\par
customers' purchase intention. These results are similar to the past e-commerce research, such
\par
as Hsiao et al.'s (2010) study in terms of social shopping that focused on the mechanism of
\par
trust in online product recommendations, and Hsu, Chang and Chuang's (2015) study of an
\par
online group-buying site that explored the influencing factors of repeat purchase intention.
\par
5.2 Theoretical contributions
\par
This study has three theoretical contributions. First of all, some prior studies have only
\par
concentrated on the technological improvement to enhance the recommendation accuracy of
\par
social recommender systems. This study aims to be one of the first to explore the impact
\par
of social recommender systems on consumer decisions from a psychological perspective.
