\par
Thus, this study enhances the understandings of the social recommender system research
\par
by exploring the effects of informational factors on purchase intention in social
\par
recommender systems. This study fills up this research gap in the social recommender
\par
system research by theorizing and empirically validating the important mechanism of
\par
informational factors, which include recommendation persuasiveness and recommendation
\par
completeness in affecting consumer purchase intention.
\par
Moreover, this study introduces the ELM to the social recommendation research. The
\par
previous studies have usually treated central route factors, i.e. information quality, as a
\par
single variable (Chavez et al., 2015; Chen et al., 2015). While this study identified two specific
\par
sets of central route factors, i.e. recommendation persuasiveness and recommendation
\par
completeness, which are relevant to trust in recommendations and perceived value. The
\par
results proved that two dimensions of information quality can significantly influence
\par
consumer attitudes with respect to recommendations.
\par
Third, this study highlights that trust in recommendations and perceived value showed
\par
significant mediating effects between informational factors and consumers' purchase
\par
intention in social recommender systems. Although the prior research has explored some of
\par
our identified factors (e.g. trust and perceived value) based on technology adoption theory,
\par
and it has mainly concentrated on their direct influence on consumers' purchase intention,
\par
our proposed model sheds new light on how informational factors will affect consumers'
\par
purchase intention through mediation pathways. Specifically, we investigate the roles of
\par
trust in recommendations and perceived value in mediating the effects of informational
\par
factors and consumer purchase intention. The current study finds that trust in
\par
recommendations and perceived value fully mediated the impact of informational factors
\par
on consumer purchase intention.
\par
5.3 Practical contributions
\par
There are several practical implications for companies that provide social recommender
\par
systems in this study. First, this study found that trust can be considered as an effective
\par
factor that impacts comembers' purchase intention. If consumers are inclined to trust
\par
recommendations that are provided by social recommender systems, they will be more
\par
likely to buy the recommended products. Therefore, it is very important for managers to
\par
establish the trustful relationship with their users and take full advantage of their resource
\par
to facilitate trust-building. Managers should support conditions that cultivate common trust
\par
among members in the social recommender systems. Moreover, our findings also suggested
\par
that perceived value is very significant in cultivating customers' purchase intention. So,
\par
managers should pay attention to consumers' perceived value regarding inducing
\par
comembers' purchase intention. A feasible way to provide the preferred value of the
\par
recommendations was to ensure the ability and knowledge of the consumer in generating
\par
the comment. Third, the findings clearly show that both recommendation persuasiveness
\par
and recommendation completeness were positively related to building trust and perceived
\par
value. So, providing persuasive and complete recommendations were effective ways to
\par
enhance trust and perceived value. The practical implication of these results is that
\par
managers should emphasize the details of the recommendation content. A persuasive and
\par
complete recommendation could help to make trust building and perceived value a less
\par
difficult task for social recommender systems to undertake, thereby supporting the
\par
successful development of e-commerce in their regions. Managers should utilize better
\par
strategies to improve the persuasiveness and completeness of recommendations and
\par
provide only strong and relevant information to consumers. For example, managers should
\par
establish a supervision system to keep the recommender information precise, current,
\par
credible and at the right level of detail, such as refining the guideline system to help
\par
consumers to write and edit their recommendations.5.4 Limitations
\par
This study has several limitations. First, it only targeted one social recommender system in
\par
China, although we think that the results should be applicable to other social recommender
\par
systems that are similar to the one that is used in WeChat. Further studies can generalize
\par
these results in various social recommender environments to determine its validity. Second,
\par
we investigate the impact of information factors, recommendation persuasiveness and
\par
recommendation completeness, on consumer decisions. Thus, further research could take
\par
more information attributes into account, such as information accuracy, information
\par
readability and information timeliness.
\par
6. Conclusions
\par
The influences of informational factors on purchase intention in social recommender
\par
systems were investigated in this study. Based on a survey data of 161 samples, we
\par
found support for our proposed model. Trust in recommendations and perceived
