\par
1) DECISION TREES (DTs)
\par
A DT models a decision problem by showing all possible
\par
combinations of decision and observations in a particular
\par
sequence on a tree structure. A DT also has decision, chance
\par
and utility nodes shown by rectangle, circle and diamond
\par
shapes respectively (see Figure 2). Each outgoing arc from a
\par
decision node represents a decision alternative, and each out-
\par
going arc from a chance node represents an outcome labelled
\par
with its name and probability. The utility nodes are located
\par
at the leaves of the tree structure and cannot have outgoing
\par
arcs. As a result, each path from the root node to a leaf node
\par
represents a decision scenario with a sequence of decisions
\par
and observations. DTs have been popular decision modelling
\par
tools due to the simplicity of their use and computation.
\par
However, the size of a DT grows exponentially as its number
\par
of variables or states increases.
\par
There is a large literature on the use of IDs as an alternative
\par
to DTs, as IDs can represent a decision problem in a more
\par
compact way than DTs [9], [15]. Figure 2 shows the DT
\par
representation of the same decision problem as Figure 1. IDs
\par
represent each decision and chance variable with a single
\par
node; whereas in a DT a variable requires multiple associated
