\par
Therefore, it is widely accepted that IDs provide a clearer and
\par
more compact representation of complex decision problems.
\par
2) MARKOV MODELS (MMs)
\par
In medical decision-making, a MM is used for evaluating
\par
the outcomes of a decision over time. A MM is composed
\par
of discrete time stages. The state of the system in a time
\par
stage is only dependent on the previous time stage. A MM
\par
is called a Hidden MM (HMM) if the state cannot be entirely
\par
observed at a time stage. Both MM and HMM models can
\par
also be represented as a Dynamic BN (DBN) [18]. A DBN
\par
is an extension of BNs that has a replicated BN structure for
\par
different time stages.
\par
Figure 3 shows a simple MM that evaluates the state of
\par
a patient over time. The patient can be `Healthy', `Sick'
\par
or `Dead', and the state of a patient at a time stage only
\par
depends on the previous time stage. Figure 4 shows a DBN
\par
representation of this model. In this example, the transition
\par
probabilities are fixed hence each node has the same NPT
\par
shown in Figure 4. The time stages in the DBN can repeated
\par
to analyze the model over a desired time period. MMs with
\par
time-dependent transition probabilities can also be modelled
\par
as DBNs that have different NPT parameters for different
\par
time stages.
\par
In clinical decision making models, MMs of out-
\par
comes are often combined with DTs to analyze the
\par
outcome of a decision over a long period of time
\par
(see Sox, et al [19, Ch. 7], and Hunink, et al. [20, Ch. 10],
\par
Fenwick, et al. [21]). It is also possible to do this by combin-
\par
ing an ID model with a DBN. For example, Figure 5 shows
\par
a combination of the ID model in Figure 1 with the DBN
\par
model in Figure 4. This model analyses the outcomes of the
\par
treatment selection model in Figure 1 over multiple years.
\par
