\par
A. EVPPI IN MONTE CARLO (MC) SAMPLING
\par
Two-level MC sampling with nested outer and inner loops
\par
can be used when the EVPPI cannot be solved analytically.
\par
In this technique, the inputs are sampled in the outer loop and
\par
the remaining parameters are sampled within the inner loop.
\par
Nested MC sampling can demand excessive computation
\par
resources and time, even for moderately sized models [2].
\par
It can also generate biased results if small sized inner samples
\par
are used [22]. The computational burden may further increase
\par
if the inputs are correlated and the conditional distributions
\par
are difficult to sample [2].
\par
Several approximation methods have been proposed
\par
to calculate the EVPPI using one-level MC sampling.
\par
Sadatsafavi, et al. [5], Strong and Oakley [3],
\par
Strong, et al. [4] use the data generated by probabilistic sensi-
\par
tivity analysis to calculate an approximate EVPPI. For analy-
\par
sis of individual parameters, Strong and Oakley [3] partition
\par
the output data into bins and calculate an approximate EVPPI.
\par
For multiple parameters, Strong, et al. [4] use the data to
\par
build a non-parametric regression model where the dependent
\par
variable is the net benefit and the independent variables are
\par
the parameters that are analyzed for the EVPPI. They used
\par
Generalized Additive Models (GAM) and Gaussian Process
\par
(GP) approaches, as flexible regression methods are required
\par
for estimating EVPPI. The methods of Sadatsafavi, et al. [5],
\par
Strong and Oakley [3] and Strong, et al. [4] have been imple-
\par
mented in the BCEA package in R [23]. Online applications
\par
for running these methods are also available but they require
\par
the results of the model to be analyzed in the form of a
\par
large set of MC or MCMC samples [24], [25]. Readers are
\par
referred to Heath, et al. [8] for a detailed review of different
\par
approximate sampling based methods for computing VOI and
\par
EVPPI [3]--[5], [7], [26].
