In summary, computation of EVPPI in IDs requires solv-
\par
ing two ID models and subtracting their expected utili-
\par
ties. There is an extensive literature on solving discrete
\par
IDs. Earlier research on this topic focused on solving
\par
IDs by marginalizing variables or transforming them to
\par
DTs or BNs [12], [27]--[30]. Jensen and Dittmer [31] modi-
\par
fied the JT algorithm for IDs, which they call a strong JT, and
\par
developed a special propagation scheme to compute expected
\par
utilities. Dittmer and Jensen [32] proposed a VOI approach
\par
to compute EVPPI directly on strong JTs. Shachter [33]
\par
focused on improving this approach by generating more
\par
efficient strong JTs for VOI, and reusing them for different
\par
EVPPI analyses. Liao and Ji [34] proposed an approach that
\par
can evaluate the combined EVPPI for a series of observa-
\par
tions in IDs with certain constraints. Since most popular BN
\par
algorithms, including JT, were designed to solve discrete
\par
models, ID algorithms that use BN conversion or strong
\par
JT only apply to discrete IDs. Solving HIDs is, how-
\par
ever, a more challenging task. Initial research on solving
\par
HIDs focused on Gaussian distributions due their convenient
\par
computational properties [35]--[37]. Cobb and Shenoy [38],
\par
Cobb and Shenoy [39] and Li and Shenoy [40] proposed a
\par
