D. FURTHER APPROXIMATION OF EVPPI USING DD
\par
The algorithm described in Section IV-B enters evidence
\par
for each state combination of the observable chance and
\par
decision nodes. The DD algorithm revises its discretizations
\par
every time evidence is entered. To calculate EVPPI of an
\par
unobservable chance variable, it needs to be transformed
\par
into an observable variable in the modified ID as described
\par
in Section IV-C. Therefore, the modified ID has an even
\par
higher number of state combinations of observable chance
\par
and decision nodes. Rather than revising the discretizations,
\par
a further approximation of our EVPPI technique uses DD
\par
only once initially, and then generates a fixed discretization
\par
of this model. The propagations, for all state combinations,
\par
in the following steps are applied to this fixed discretization
\par
by using only JT. This means observations entered in to the
\par
model preserve the prior discretized points on all unobserved
\par
variables. We call this approximation DD with Fixed dis-
\par
cretization (DD-Fixed).
\par
As we show later in Section V, DD-Fixed works faster
\par
but is also less accurate than DD because the discretizations
\par
are not optimized for the posteriors in each iteration. In the
\par
following section, we show another approximation that com-
\par
putes only the expected utility and EVPPI values rather than
\par
the whole distribution of them.
