E. EXTERNAL CALCULATION OF UTILITY FUNCTIONS
\par
In health economics models, the utility function is a deter-
\par
ministic function of random variables such as costs and life
\par
expectancy. The DD algorithm can solve hybrid models with
\par
all common conditional deterministic functions of random
\par
variables. It computes the entire probability distribution of
\par
such functions and thus it enables the decision maker to
\par
assess the uncertainty of the model's estimates as well as the
\par
expected values.
\par
In an HID, deterministic utility functions are modelled as
\par
a child node to all the variables of that function. Therefore,
\par
this node has many parents if the utility function has many
\par
variables. The computational complexity of standard propa-
\par
gation algorithms, such as JT, depends on the largest clique
\par
size, and the clique size can explode if a variable has many
\par
parents. Although solutions have been proposed for this [46],
\par
solving a HID that has a utility node with many parents can
\par
still be slow or infeasible.
\par
If a decision maker is interested in the expected value of
\par
additional information, computing the expected values of the
\par
utility function, rather than the entire probability distribution,
\par
is sufficient. In this case, we can calculate the posteriors
\par
of the marginal probability distributions of the independent
\par
variables and joint probability distribution of the dependent
\par
variables in the utility function, and then we can simply sum
\par
and multiply the expected values of the variables externally.
\par
For example, in Figure 8, the utility functions associated with
\par
each treatment alternative are `1000\texttimes RT\_A -- 4000\texttimes SE\_A''
\par
and `1000\texttimes RT\_B -- 4000\texttimes SE\_B' respectively. Since RT\_A
\par
and SE\_A, and RT\_B and SE\_B are respectively independent
\par
of each other, we can simply calculate their posterior distri-
\par
bution, and then apply the utility functions to the expected
\par
values obtained from these variables. We only calculate the
\par
expected values of NB, rather than the whole distribution,
\par
resulting in much faster computation of EVPPI.
\par
