\par
However, the main advantage of our method is its usability
\par
as it is based on discretized posterior distributions that are
\par
automatically handled by the DD algorithm which is imple-
\par
mented in a widely available tool. Therefore, in contrast to
\par
the other approaches, it does not require users to assess the
\par
convergence of sampling.
\par
VI. CONCLUSIONS
\par
This paper presented a novel technique to calculate the
\par
EVPPI of individual continuous variables in IDs, and an
\par
approximation of this technique to trade-off accuracy for
\par
speed. We demonstrated the use of this technique and
\par
applied it to two case studies that were used in simi-
\par
lar studies. We compared the results of our approach to
\par
five other general techniques used for computing EVPPI,
\par
namely those of Brennan, et al. [2], Strong and Oakley [3],
\par
Sadatsafavi, et al. [5], and the GP and GAM regression meth-
\par
ods of Strong, et al. [4]. While all previous techniques use
\par
sampling to calculate EVPPI, our approach uses an entirely
\par
different technique that dynamically discretizes all continu-
\par
ous variables, and calculates the posteriors by using a popular
\par
Bayesian inference algorithm called JT. As a result, it can
\par
handle a large class of models with virtually any kind of
\par
continuous distribution. Our approach successfully calcu-
\par
lated EVPPIs for individual variables in both case studies.
\par
In contrast to the previous techniques, our approach can be
\par
used by decision-makers who do not have deep understanding
\par
of programming languages and sampling techniques, since it
\par
offers a simpler way of calculating EVPPI. The case studies
\par
show that, while our approach requires longer computation
\par
times, there is no compromise on EVPPI accuracy.
\par
Our technique uses a powerful inference algorithm that
\par
is readily implemented in a commercial tool with a user-
\par
friendly graphical interface. Application of the technique
\par
requires only simple graphical operations on the ID models
\par
and computing these models by using the proposed algo-
\par
rithms. As further research, the proposed approach could be
\par
extended to calculate the EVPPI of a group of parameters.
\par
This could be achieved by adding multiple information arcs
\par
on an ID and computing the difference between expected util-
\par
ities. An automated implementation of the EVPPI algorithm
\par
and the proposed approximations would enable a wider use of
\par
these techniques by clinicians and domain experts. We also
\par
plan to further evaluate the general accuracy of DD-Fixed
\par
approximation in HBN and HID models.
\par
