\par
for suppliers due to the rapid development of the logistics industry. After the 1990s, the measure
\par
of supplier selection received more attention when some scholars refined partial factors including
\par
purchased price, product quality, and delivery time, and further proposed second-grade and even
\par
third-grade indicators in order to make assessments quantifiable and comparable [10,46,48]. The cost
\par
(or purchase unit price), for instance, was subdivided into fixed costs, design costs, supply costs,
\par
technical expenses, after-sales service costs, and inventory costs, and so forth. Later on, with the
\par
view of Business Process Reengineering (BPR) causing great repercussions [53], capabilities including
\par
resource integration, supply flexibility, and information technology have received unprecedented
\par
attention [54], and were also added to the supplier evaluation system for core enterprises to measure
\par
their suppliers' performance. In the face of the competitive environment that came with economic
\par
globalization, the ability and responsibility of suppliers are expected to remain at a much higher
\par
level, in terms of factors such as compliance with local regulations, assuming client responsibilities
\par
proactively, identifying supply chain risks, and so forth. As global attention has turned to sustainable
\par
development [55] and Green supply chain management (GSCM) [56], environmental performance has
\par
gradually become a crucial factor in the selection of potential suppliers (especially green suppliers),
\par
while social performance was hardly mentioned until corporate social responsibility (CSR) [31] and
\par
the TBL theory [19] drew much attention to it. Some scholars have focused on this issue but have not
\par
yet established a generally accepted measure for supplier social responsibility [7,9,17,18,57].
\par
Based on the three dimensions in the TBL framework [19], indicators affecting supplier selection
\par
in the last 20 years or so were classified into the economic, environmental, and societal metrics.
\par
The research on traditional supplier evaluation generally only concerned economic performance [45,58--
\par
61]. Table 2 summarizes a segment of the most frequent economic indicators in the previous literature.
\par
With increasing emphasis on global environmental issues and the enhancement of ecological awareness,
\par
it was not surprising that environmental performance has become the essential assessment indicator for
\par
suppliers in the selection system [13,14,62--65]. Table 3 summarizes the environmental indicators that
\par
have been widely recognized and used in the past. Although the study on CSR has been not a new topic
\par
and has already borne fruit, research on integrating CSR (and also sustainability) with supplier selection
\par
has emerged only in recent years [63,66,67]. In reality, the internal stakeholders (such as shareholders)
\par
and external stakeholders (such as governments, local communities, non-governmental organizations,
\par
and consumers) in the supply chain are constantly putting social responsibility pressure on the firm
\par
itself [66,68]. Corporate behaviors and practices in terms of social responsibility, such as the voluntary
\par
integration of sustainable concerns into their commercial operations, influence all levels of the supply
\par
chain, but especially business partners [69]. Enterprises gradually recognized that their initiatives in
\par
SSCM will eventually exert an impact on their social image, reputation, and long-term benefits [70],
\par
and are responsible for the environmental, well-being, and security requirements of the laborers who
\par
manufacture their products, no matter whether they are direct employees or whether they come from
\par
the suppliers who provide them with raw materials or semi-finished products [71]. Table 4 provides
\par
some important sustainable criteria from the existing literature. Most authors have emphasized concern
\par
for employee responsibility, in the form of occupational health and safety [4,15--17,72], training and
\par
practice [15,18], and other legitimate rights [4,15]. The rights of stakeholders have also been recognized
\par
by many researchers [4,7,29,72], but the concept is more general because groups such as laborers,
\par
shareholders, governments, and so forth are all stakeholders of the company, and it is obviously not
\par
sufficient to describe rights using such an indicator. Information disclosure reveals the willingness of
\par
enterprises to fulfill their social responsibilities to some degree [4,29], but it is usually hard to measure
\par
performance by a single factor. Few papers pay attention to external stakeholders, except for their
\par
influence on the local community [73,74]; criteria like taxation, charitable investments, and shareholderSustainability 2018, 10, 2543 6 of 49
\par
contracts, are rarely included in the evaluation systems of sustainable suppliers. Nevertheless, the
\par
indicators proposed in the past three years, such as ethical and legal issues [17], discrimination and
\par
diversity [7], sustainability risks [57], supportive activities [16], and trust and partnerships [18], have
\par
some reference values. In general, the current social dimension for sustainable suppliers lacks the
\par
systematic consideration of responsibilities to internal and external stakeholders, focusing rather on
\par
only one or a few objects. This paper therefore attempts to establish a novel criteria framework of SSS
\par
for retail enterprises, especially those existing in the emerging economy whose SSCM initiatives got
\par
off to a late start.
\par
2.3. The Measures
\par
Through reviewing and combing through over 300 papers published from 2000 to 2017 (on
\par
the ``topic, abstract and keywords'' in regards to ``sustainable supplier'') from databases such as
\par
the Web of Science, Springer, Wiley, and Scopus, as well as in-depth consultations with experts in
\par
various industries, based on the TBL theory and stakeholder theory, a comprehensive measure for
\par
SSS in the retail industry was formulated. Given the trade-off between economic, environmental, and
\par
societal objectives, this measure covers six aspects (and a total of 24 sub-criteria), namely: Corporate
\par
Reputation (AS1), Operational Management (AS2), Product Advantage (AS3), Service Capability
\par
(AS4), Green Impact (AS5), and Social Responsibility (AS6). One of the most innovative parts among
\par
these aspects is the introduction of the main content of supplier social responsibility, which is not
\par
only reflected in suppliers' duties to internal stakeholders (such as employees and shareholders),
\par
but also to external stakeholders (such as community and the government). Among the sub-criteria,
\par
in addition to the reservation of some traditional, highly cited criteria such as procurement costs,
\par
financial status, product quality, supply flexibility, responsiveness, etc., we also considered other,
\par
new indicators according to corporate practices and expert opinion, including position in industry,
\par
contractual capacity, strategic alliance, learning, business process management, customer satisfaction,
\par
maintenance and compensation, governmental relations, and shareholder contracts; explanations
\par
are presented in Table 5. The proposed measure attempts to objectively reflect the actual capabilities
\par
and potential of alternative suppliers to maintain the long-term benefits of the supply chain through
\par
cooperation with partners.Sustainability 2018, 10, 2543 7 of 49
