2.4. Supplier Selection Methods
\par
Approaches to supplier selection have gone through three main stages of evolution [120]. Early
\par
studies on supplier selection normally adopted qualitative methods such as the Heuristics of Judgment,
\par
Bidding, and Consultation Choice [121]. However, due to the overdependence on the subjectiveSustainability 2018, 10, 2543 10 of 49
\par
judgment of DMs, this type of method was less accepted by subsequent procurement managers. They
\par
instead tried to use quantitative methods, such as the Entropy method, to get easy access to the relative
\par
weights of the criteria for ranking their suppliers [122]. Nonetheless, authors later found that many
\par
criteria, like ``strategic alliance'' and ``innovation intention'', could not be directly quantified. Though
\par
some of their sub-criteria may be quantifiable, they could not entirely represent or replace the concepts
\par
of the original criteria. Combination methods (quantitative and qualitative) have, therefore, appeared
\par
and become the mainstream in field of supplier selection. Currently, models and methods that were
\par
applied generally include the following four categories: (a) MCDM methods (for example, AHP, ANP,
\par
TOPSIS, VIKOR, and so forth); (b) Mathematical Programming (MP) models (for example, DEA, Linear
\par
Programming (LP), Nonlinear Programming (NLP), Multi-objective Programming (MOP), and so
\par
forth); (c) Artificial Intelligence Technology (AIT) (for example, Grey Relation Analysis (GRA), ANN,
\par
Case-based Reasoning (CBR), Decision Support Systems (DSS), and so forth); and (d) Other Hybrid
\par
Models which are basically an integration of the above methods that assembled unique advantages
\par
of each technique and provided more sophisticated structures to assess supplier performance [7,123].
\par
Moreover, from fuzzy set theory, introducing fuzzy numbers with various forms to characterize the
\par
uncertainty of information is also quite commonly adopted in this field [124]. Table 6 shows the diverse
\par
extended forms of typical portfolio models used in the supplier selection area.
