MCDM, a centralized decision-making method for ranking and choosing the optimal
\par
alternative in limited (infinite) programs under a conflicting and non-commensurable situation,
\par
has wide applications in many fields, including engineering, military, economy, technology, and
\par
management [5,6,72]. In the multiple criteria framework for supplier selection, single-goal methods
\par
would hardly solve the problem, as cost indicators and benefit indicators are often contradictory.
\par
MCDM methods such as the TOPSIS and VIKOR techniques, relying on consolidation function
\par
measuring the ``closeness to the ideal solution'', are proved to be typical compromise programming
\par
technologies [24,151] which can logically and effectively address SSS issues with multiple objectives.
\par
Compared with a large number of the extensions of the TOPSIS method in the past decade, the
\par
improved forms of the VIKOR algorithm have just appeared in recent years. However, VIKOR is
\par
technically superior to TOPSIS, mainly because the final optimal solution obtained by VIKOR is closer
\par
to the ideal solution than that of TOPSIS [24].
\par
Before using the VIKOR algorithm to acquire priority ranking, criteria weights need to be
\par
determined first. Expert experience and the weighted average method are universally used in
\par
reality [28], but such methods barely consider possible judgment biases that DMs may make, and
\par
rarely inspect any potential inconsistencies within the model [12]. The AHP, ANP, and ANN methods
\par
have been applied to address such limitations; however, these methods assume that indicators are
\par
internally independent, and therefore, ignore the possible interdependencies among them [49,63],
\par
which may yield erroneous weights. As the relationships between calculated weights can deviate
\par
from the actual phenomenon, scholars for this concern have considered combining the DEMATEL
\par
method with the ANP to calculate weights [28,76,152]. DEMATEL can analyze the interplay among
\par
the evaluation indexes and obtain a more intuitive index network structure which may help to redefine
\par
the relationship between the first-and second-level indexes under ANP, so as to acquire more accurate
\par
and objective criteria weights. This weight acquisition approach has seldom been applied in SSS, and
\par
existing studies almost always stop at the combination of the DEMATEL, ANP and VIKOR methods,
\par
rarely expanding further.
\par
On the other hand, as the input of VIKOR, the value of indicators is not always easy to
\par
observe directly due to the complexity and uncertainty of the SSS problem. Fuzzy number is
\par
therefore introduced to be an effective extension to characterize the vagueness and uncertainty in
\par
the data process [81]. Interval number [152,153], triangular fuzzy number [25,154], trapezoidal fuzzy
\par
number [12], intuitionistic fuzzy number [155], two-tuple linguistic information [156], generalized
\par
interval trapezoidal fuzzy number [157], and other different forms of fuzzy number have been
\par
widely applied to be information aggregation under the VIKOR method, but there are hardly any
\par
applications introducing IVTFN into the VIKOR method, and this fuzzy number is seldom used to
\par
describe the characteristics of indicators for sustainable suppliers. As Kuo and Liang [27] and Lin and
\par
Tseng [26] have verified the applicability and effectiveness of the IVTFN in performance evaluation,
\par
we thereby consider introducing this data type into the SSS problem as the semantic expression of
